Java · array · complexity · L3
Concept: int arithmetic wraps silently; use long for sums and a safe midpoint
Misconception: If a program does not throw, its integer arithmetic is correct.
Explanation: Java int values wrap around past 2,147,483,647 without any error. (lo + hi) / 2 becomes negative when lo + hi is too large, which breaks binary search on huge arrays, and running sums of large values come out wrong. Compute the midpoint as lo + (hi - lo) / 2, store sums and products in long, and compare with Integer.compare instead of subtracting. JavaScript solutions do not hit this because their numbers are doubles.
Incorrect:
int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
int sum = 0;
for (int v : nums) sum += v;
Correct:
int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
long sum = 0;
for (int v : nums) sum += v;
Arrays.sort(boxed, Integer::compare);
Complexity: No change in complexity; correctness only

