Java · bits · iteration · L2
Concept: Count or visit set bits with while (n != 0) and n &= n - 1
Misconception: A loop over the set bits of n can use while (n > 0).
Explanation: When bit 31 is set, an int is negative, so while (n > 0) never runs and reports zero set bits. LeetCode's Number of 1 Bits passes such values as negative ints. Loop while n != 0 instead. n &= n - 1 clears the lowest set bit, so the loop runs once per set bit and works for any sign. Integer.bitCount(n) gives the same count with a single fast instruction.
Incorrect:
int count = 0;
while (n > 0) { n &= n - 1; count++; } // 0 for any n with bit 31 set
Correct:
static int hammingWeight(int n) {
  int count = 0;
  while (n != 0) {
    n &= n - 1;
    count++;
  }
  return count; // equals Integer.bitCount(n)
}
Complexity: O(number of set bits)

