Java · bits · access · L2
Concept: x & -x isolates the lowest set bit; x & (x - 1) clears it
Misconception: Finding the lowest set bit requires a loop over every position.
Explanation: In two's complement, -x equals ~x + 1: every bit above the lowest set bit of x flips, and that bit and the zeros below it stay the same. So x & -x keeps only the lowest set bit. Subtracting 1 flips the lowest set bit and every zero below it, so x & (x - 1) clears that bit. These two expressions drive set-bit iteration, power-of-two checks and Fenwick trees, whose blocks have length i & -i. Both give 0 when x is 0, so loops must stop there.
Incorrect:
int low = 1;
while ((x & low) == 0) low <<= 1; // loops forever when x == 0
Correct:
static List<Integer> setBitPositions(int x) {
  List<Integer> positions = new ArrayList<>();
  while (x != 0) {
    positions.add(Integer.numberOfTrailingZeros(x & -x));
    x &= x - 1;
  }
  return positions;
}
Complexity: O(1) per expression; O(set bits) to visit them all

