Java · bits · patterns · L3
Concept: XOR removes values that appear in pairs, leaving the unpaired one
Misconception: Finding the single unpaired value needs a HashSet or a count map.
Explanation: XOR is associative and commutative, x ^ x is 0, and x ^ 0 is x. XOR-ing every value in an array therefore cancels every value that appears twice, in any order, and leaves the one that appears once, in O(n) time and O(1) space. Missing Number uses the same idea by XOR-ing every index 0..n with every value: the indices and values pair up except for the missing number.
Incorrect:
Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
for (int v : nums) if (!seen.add(v)) seen.remove(v); // works, but O(n) extra space
Correct:
static int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
  int x = nums.length;
  for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) x ^= i ^ nums[i];
  return x;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

