Two snippets cover printing and the unsigned view.

```java
// 1. Print an int as exactly 32 bits, grouped in fours for readability.
static String bits32(int x) {
    String s = String.format("%32s", Integer.toBinaryString(x)).replace(' ', '0');
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (int i = 0; i < 32; i += 4) sb.append(s, i, i + 4).append(i < 28 ? "_" : "");
    return sb.toString();
}
// bits32(-8) -> "1111_1111_1111_1111_1111_1111_1111_1000"

// 2. The same 32 bits, read as signed and as unsigned.
int x = -8;
long unsigned = Integer.toUnsignedLong(x); // 4294967288
int logical = x >>> 28;                    // 15: zeros shifted in
int arithmetic = x >> 28;                  // -1: sign bits shifted in
```

Snippet 1 is the most useful debugging helper in this blueprint: almost every bit bug becomes obvious once both operands are printed as full 32-bit strings.

