Java · object · equality · L2
Concept: Override equals(Object) and hashCode together, over the same fields
Misconception: Overriding equals is enough for a class to work as a HashMap key or HashSet element.
Explanation: HashMap and HashSet first use hashCode to pick a bucket, then equals to compare within it. If two objects are equal but return different hash codes, they land in different buckets and are never compared, so the set keeps duplicates and lookups miss. Override equals(Object), not equals(MyType), mark it @Override, and compute hashCode from exactly the fields equals uses. Records generate both correctly.
Incorrect:
final class P {
  final int x, y;
  P(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
  public boolean equals(P o) { return x == o.x && y == o.y; } // overload, no hashCode
}
Correct:
final class P {
  final int x, y;
  P(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
  @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    return o instanceof P p && x == p.x && y == p.y;
  }
  @Override public int hashCode() { return 31 * x + y; }
}
Complexity: O(fields) per call

