Java · Java Classes, Records and Object Equality · Equality and hashing track
Three snippets cover the contracts learners must be able to write correctly.

```java
// 1. A record as a value-based key: equals, hashCode and toString come for free.
record Cell(int r, int c) {}

Set<Cell> seen = new HashSet<>();
seen.add(new Cell(2, 3));
boolean found = seen.contains(new Cell(2, 3)); // true

// 2. A hand-written key class: override both methods over the same fields.
final class Pair {
    private final int a;
    private final int b;

    Pair(int a, int b) {
        this.a = a;
        this.b = b;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Pair p)) return false; // pattern matching for instanceof (Java 16)
        return a == p.a && b == p.b;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return 31 * a + b;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "(" + a + ", " + b + ")";
    }
}

// 3. Ordering objects with comparator factories, ties broken by a sequence number.
record Task(int priority, long seq, String name) {}

PriorityQueue<Task> queue = new PriorityQueue<>(
    Comparator.comparingInt(Task::priority).thenComparingLong(Task::seq));
```

Snippet 1 is the modern answer to the most common Java grid and graph question, "how do I put a coordinate in a `HashSet`?": a two-line record replaces both the unreliable `int[]` key and the hand-written `Pair` class.

