Java · Java Classes, Records and Object Equality · Object model and design track
Three snippets show the shapes learners write most: a node type, a design class with an invariant, and an immutable value type.

```java
// 1. Node types: static nested, so they need no outer instance.
class Solution {
    static class Node {
        int val;
        Node next;

        Node(int val) {
            this.val = val; // not "val = val"
        }
    }
}

// 2. A design class: private state, and the invariant restored in one method.
class MedianFinder {
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> low = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.reverseOrder()); // smaller half
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> high = new PriorityQueue<>();                         // larger half

    public void addNum(int x) {
        low.offer(x);
        high.offer(low.poll());                              // every low value <= every high value
        if (high.size() > low.size()) low.offer(high.poll()); // low is equal or one larger
    }

    public double findMedian() {
        return low.size() > high.size() ? low.peek() : (low.peek() + (double) high.peek()) / 2;
    }
}

// 3. An immutable value type with validation.
record Interval(int start, int end) {
    Interval {
        if (end < start) throw new IllegalArgumentException("end before start");
    }

    boolean overlaps(Interval other) {
        return start <= other.end && other.start <= end;
    }
}
```

Snippet 2 follows the two-heap approach of the corpus's Find Median from Data Stream solution; the two `private final` heaps are the point of the lesson, since the invariant cannot be broken from outside the class.

