Java · collections · mental-model · L0
Concept: Declare collections by interface and choose the implementation separately
Misconception: The variable's type should be the concrete class, such as ArrayList or HashMap.
Explanation: Java's collections are organised as interfaces (List, Set, Queue, Deque, Map) with several implementations each. Declaring List<Integer> xs = new ArrayList<>() lets the rest of the code depend only on list operations, so switching to another implementation changes one line. Choose the interface from the operations the algorithm needs, then the implementation from their costs: ArrayList, HashSet, HashMap, ArrayDeque and PriorityQueue are the defaults.
Incorrect:
ArrayList<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
LinkedList<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>(); // concrete types everywhere, and a slow queue
Correct:
static List<Integer> levelValues(TreeNode root) {
  List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
  Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
  if (root != null) queue.offer(root);
  while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    TreeNode node = queue.poll();
    result.add(node.val);
    if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
    if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
  }
  return result;
}
Complexity: No runtime cost; it changes only the declared type

