Java · collections · edge-cases · L1
Concept: Each collection has its own null rule; check it before storing null
Misconception: Every Java collection accepts null elements, keys and values.
Explanation: ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet and HashMap accept null (HashMap allows one null key and any number of null values). ArrayDeque and PriorityQueue reject null, because their poll and peek return null to mean "empty". TreeSet and TreeMap reject null keys under natural ordering, ConcurrentHashMap rejects null keys and values, and List.of, Set.of, Map.of and the copyOf methods reject null entirely. Avoid storing null in collections; use sentinels or Optional return values instead.
Incorrect:
Deque<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
queue.offer(node.left); // NullPointerException when left is null
Correct:
static void offerChildren(Deque<TreeNode> queue, TreeNode node) {
  if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
  if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
}
Complexity: O(1)

