Java · graph · creation · L1
Concept: One new list per node, and both directions for undirected edges
Misconception: Collections.nCopies(n, new ArrayList<>()) creates n empty neighbour lists, and each edge only needs to be stored once.
Explanation: nCopies and Arrays.fill store the same list object n times, so every node ends up with every neighbour. A generic array (new ArrayList[n]) starts with null slots. Create one list per node in a loop. In an undirected graph, edge [u, v] must be added to both u's and v's lists, or traversals cannot move from v back to u and component counts come out too high.
Incorrect:
List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>(Collections.nCopies(n, new ArrayList<>()));
for (int[] e : edges) adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]); // shared list, one direction
Correct:
static List<List<Integer>> buildUndirected(int n, int[][] edges) {
  List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
  for (int[] e : edges) {
    adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);
    adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);
  }
  return adj;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time and space

