Java · graph · patterns · L2
Concept: Track nodes on the current path, not just visited nodes
Misconception: In a directed graph, reaching an already-visited node during DFS means there is a cycle.
Explanation: Two paths can reach the same node in a directed acyclic graph, as in a diamond. Reaching a node that is already finished is harmless; only reaching a node still on the current path means a cycle. Keep an int[] state with 0 for unvisited, 1 for on the current path and 2 for finished, and start a DFS from every unvisited node. For graphs with very long paths, convert the recursion to an explicit ArrayDeque.
Incorrect:
static boolean dfs(List<List<Integer>> adj, int u, boolean[] visited) {
  if (visited[u]) return true; // reports the diamond as a cycle
  visited[u] = true;
  for (int v : adj.get(u)) if (dfs(adj, v, visited)) return true;
  return false;
}
Correct:
static boolean hasCycle(List<List<Integer>> adj) {
  int[] state = new int[adj.size()];
  for (int i = 0; i < adj.size(); i++) if (state[i] == 0 && dfs(adj, i, state)) return true;
  return false;
}
static boolean dfs(List<List<Integer>> adj, int u, int[] state) {
  state[u] = 1;
  for (int v : adj.get(u)) {
    if (state[v] == 1) return true;
    if (state[v] == 0 && dfs(adj, v, state)) return true;
  }
  state[u] = 2;
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

