Java · graph · patterns · L3
Concept: Merge roots, not nodes; a failed union means the edge closes a cycle
Misconception: Merging two groups means setting parent[a] = b.
Explanation: Union-find keeps each group as a tree identified by its root, stored in an int[] parent. Merging must link the roots found by find, or unrelated groups get mixed up. Path halving in find and attaching the smaller tree under the larger keep operations nearly O(1). union returning false means both nodes were already connected, so the new edge closes a cycle; decrementing a counter only on successful unions gives the number of groups.
Incorrect:
void union(int a, int b) { parent[a] = b; } // links nodes, not roots
Correct:
final class UnionFind {
  private final int[] parent, size;
  private int count;
  UnionFind(int n) {
    parent = new int[n];
    size = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { parent[i] = i; size[i] = 1; }
    count = n;
  }
  int find(int x) {
    while (parent[x] != x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
    return x;
  }
  boolean union(int a, int b) {
    int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
    if (ra == rb) return false;
    if (size[ra] < size[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }
    parent[rb] = ra;
    size[ra] += size[rb];
    count--;
    return true;
  }
  int count() { return count; }
}
Complexity: Nearly O(1) amortised per operation, O(n) space

