Java · graph · equality · L3
Concept: Map each original Node to its copy, and create each copy exactly once
Misconception: A graph can be deep-copied by recursively copying each node's neighbours.
Explanation: Graphs have cycles, so naive recursive copying never ends, and shared neighbours get copied several times. Keep a HashMap<Node, Node> from each original to its copy. Clone Graph's Node class does not override equals or hashCode, so the map compares nodes by identity, which is exactly right here. Create a copy the first time a node is seen, then wire each copied node's neighbour list from the map.
Incorrect:
Node copy(Node n) {
  Node c = new Node(n.val);
  for (Node nb : n.neighbors) c.neighbors.add(copy(nb)); // loops forever on cycles
  return c;
}
Correct:
public Node cloneGraph(Node node) {
  if (node == null) return null;
  Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>();
  Deque<Node> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
  copies.put(node, new Node(node.val));
  queue.offer(node);
  while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    Node cur = queue.poll();
    for (Node nb : cur.neighbors) {
      if (!copies.containsKey(nb)) {
        copies.put(nb, new Node(nb.val));
        queue.offer(nb);
      }
      copies.get(cur).neighbors.add(copies.get(nb));
    }
  }
  return copies.get(node);
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time and space

