```java
// 3. Iterative DFS over every node: count connected components.
static int countComponents(List<List<Integer>> adj) {
    boolean[] seen = new boolean[adj.size()];
    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    int count = 0;
    for (int s = 0; s < adj.size(); s++) {
        if (seen[s]) continue;
        count++;
        seen[s] = true;
        stack.push(s);
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            int u = stack.pop();
            for (int v : adj.get(u)) if (!seen[v]) { seen[v] = true; stack.push(v); }
        }
    }
    return count;
}

// 4. Labelled nodes: a Map-based adjacency list.
static Map<String, List<String>> buildLabelled(List<List<String>> pairs) {
    Map<String, List<String>> adj = new HashMap<>();
    for (List<String> p : pairs) {
        adj.computeIfAbsent(p.get(0), k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(p.get(1));
        adj.computeIfAbsent(p.get(1), k -> new ArrayList<>()); // keep sink nodes as keys too
    }
    return adj;
}
```

Snippet 4's second `computeIfAbsent` is easy to forget: without it, nodes with no outgoing edges are missing from the map, and loops over `adj.keySet()` skip them.

