Java · grid · complexity · L4
Concept: Flood fill with an explicit ArrayDeque to avoid StackOverflowError
Misconception: Recursive DFS flood fill is safe on any grid that fits in memory.
Explanation: A recursive flood fill uses one stack frame per cell on its current path, and a large snake-shaped or solid region can make that path hundreds of thousands of cells long, which throws StackOverflowError. An explicit ArrayDeque of cells has no such limit. Mark cells when pushing them (in a boolean[][] or by overwriting the board), so no cell is pushed twice.
Incorrect:
static void sink(char[][] g, int r, int c) {
  if (r < 0 || r >= g.length || c < 0 || c >= g[0].length || g[r][c] != '1') return;
  g[r][c] = '0';
  sink(g, r + 1, c); sink(g, r - 1, c); sink(g, r, c + 1); sink(g, r, c - 1);
}
Correct:
static void sink(char[][] g, int sr, int sc) {
  int m = g.length, n = g[0].length;
  Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
  g[sr][sc] = '0';
  stack.push(new int[] {sr, sc});
  while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
    int[] cell = stack.pop();
    for (int[] d : new int[][] {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}}) {
      int r = cell[0] + d[0], c = cell[1] + d[1];
      if (r >= 0 && r < m && c >= 0 && c < n && g[r][c] == '1') {
        g[r][c] = '0'; // mark when pushed
        stack.push(new int[] {r, c});
      }
    }
  }
}
Complexity: O(m · n) time, O(m · n) heap space in the worst case

