Java · heap · iteration · L1
Concept: Only poll returns elements in priority order
Misconception: Printing or iterating a PriorityQueue shows its elements in sorted order.
Explanation: PriorityQueue stores a binary heap in an array, and toString and iterators walk that array. The heap only guarantees that each parent comes before its children, so the first element is the smallest but the rest are in heap order. To read elements in priority order, poll until the heap is empty (which removes them), or copy and sort.
Incorrect:
PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(List.of(3, 1, 2));
System.out.println(pq); // [1, 3, 2], not [1, 2, 3]
Correct:
static List<Integer> inOrder(Collection<Integer> values) {
  PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(values);
  List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>();
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) out.add(pq.poll());
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) to drain

