Java · heap · patterns · L3
Concept: Keep the k largest in a size-k min-heap
Misconception: The k largest values belong in a max-heap.
Explanation: A size-k heap must make it cheap to evict the weakest of the k items kept. For the k largest, the weakest is the smallest, which a min-heap keeps at its head: offer each value and poll when the size exceeds k. The head is then the k-th largest. The same reasoning gives a max-heap for the k smallest or the k closest. This takes O(n log k) time and O(k) memory, and also works on streams.
Incorrect:
PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());
for (int x : nums) { heap.offer(x); if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll(); } // keeps the k smallest
Correct:
static int kthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
  PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
  for (int x : nums) {
    heap.offer(x);
    if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
  }
  return heap.peek();
}
Complexity: O(n log k) time, O(k) space

