Java · heap · mutation · L4
Concept: Re-offer instead of editing an element's priority in place
Misconception: Updating a field of an object inside a PriorityQueue updates its position.
Explanation: The heap positions an element only when it is offered or when neighbouring elements move. Changing a field the comparator reads leaves the element where it was, so later polls return elements out of order without any error. PriorityQueue has no decrease-key: either remove the element (O(n)), change it and offer it again, or offer a new entry and skip stale ones when they are polled, as Dijkstra does. When priorities change often, a TreeMap or TreeSet with a tie-breaker supports O(log n) updates.
Incorrect:
task.priority = 0; // task is already in the heap; it will not move to the front
Correct:
record Entry(int dist, int node) {}
static void relax(PriorityQueue<Entry> heap, int[] dist, int v, int nd) {
  if (nd < dist[v]) {
    dist[v] = nd;
    heap.offer(new Entry(nd, v)); // a new entry; the old one becomes stale and is skipped
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n) per new entry; stale entries cost one extra poll each

