    int pop() {
        if (size == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        int top = a[0], x = a[--size];
        int i = 0;
        while (true) { // sift down: move smaller children up into the hole
            int c = 2 * i + 1;
            if (c >= size) break;
            if (c + 1 < size && a[c + 1] < a[c]) c++;
            if (a[c] >= x) break;
            a[i] = a[c];
            i = c;
        }
        if (size > 0) a[i] = x;
        return top;
    }

    int peek() {
        if (size == 0) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        return a[0];
    }

    int size() { return size; }
}
```

Snippet 4 uses the "hole" form of sifting, which moves values instead of swapping them; it is shorter and faster than swap-based sifting, and the comments make the idea explicit for learners.

