Concept: Reverse with three pointers, saving next before rewiring
Misconception: A recursive reversal is as safe as an iterative one.
Explanation: The iterative reversal keeps prev, cur and next. Saving cur.next before overwriting it is essential: once cur.next points backwards, the rest of the list is unreachable. The recursive version uses one stack frame per node, and Java performs no tail-call optimisation, so it throws StackOverflowError on lists of tens of thousands of nodes. Use the iterative version by default.
Incorrect:
static ListNode reverse(ListNode head) {
  if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;
  ListNode rest = reverse(head.next); // one frame per node
  head.next.next = head;
  head.next = null;
  return rest;
}
Correct:
static ListNode reverse(ListNode head) {
  ListNode prev = null, cur = head;
  while (cur != null) {
    ListNode next = cur.next;
    cur.next = prev;
    prev = cur;
    cur = next;
  }
  return prev;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space iteratively; O(n) stack recursively

