Java · map · ordering · L3
Concept: A LinkedHashMap in access order with removeEldestEntry is an LRU cache
Misconception: new LinkedHashMap<>() evicts the least recently used entry.
Explanation: LinkedHashMap keeps a linked list through its entries. By default it uses insertion order, so evicting its eldest entry removes the oldest inserted key. Passing true as the third constructor argument switches to access order, where get and put move an entry to the end. Overriding removeEldestEntry to return size() > capacity then evicts the least recently used entry automatically after each put.
Incorrect:
Map<Integer, Integer> cache = new LinkedHashMap<>(); // insertion order: evicts oldest inserted
Correct:
class LRUCache extends LinkedHashMap<Integer, Integer> {
  private final int capacity;
  LRUCache(int capacity) {
    super(capacity, 0.75f, true); // access order
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }
  public int get(int key) { return super.getOrDefault(key, -1); }
  public void put(int key, int value) { super.put(key, value); }
  @Override
  protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> eldest) {
    return size() > capacity;
  }
}
Complexity: O(1) average per get and put

