Java · stack · patterns · L3
Concept: A stack of indices with decreasing values finds every next-greater element in O(n)
Misconception: Finding the next greater element for every position needs nested loops.
Explanation: Keep a Deque<Integer> of indices whose values decrease from bottom to top. For each new index, pop every index whose value is smaller: the new value is their next greater element. Then push the new index. Each index is pushed and popped at most once, so the pass is O(n). Storing indices rather than values keeps distances available, as Daily Temperatures needs.
Incorrect:
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
    if (t[j] > t[i]) { res[i] = j - i; break; } // O(n^2)
Correct:
static int[] dailyTemperatures(int[] t) {
  int[] res = new int[t.length];
  Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
  for (int i = 0; i < t.length; i++) {
    while (!stack.isEmpty() && t[stack.peek()] < t[i]) {
      int j = stack.pop();
      res[j] = i - j;
    }
    stack.push(i);
  }
  return res;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

