Java · queue · patterns · L6
Concept: A BlockingQueue hands work between threads, waiting when it is full or empty
Misconception: A shared ArrayList with a busy-waiting loop is enough for a producer and a consumer.
Explanation: BlockingQueue implementations such as ArrayBlockingQueue (bounded) and LinkedBlockingQueue are thread-safe queues with waiting built in: put waits while the queue is full and take waits while it is empty. add throws IllegalStateException on a full bounded queue and offer returns false. A sentinel value (a "poison pill") tells the consumer to stop.
Incorrect:
while (shared.isEmpty()) { } // busy-waits and races with the producer
Correct:
static List<Integer> runPipeline() throws InterruptedException {
  BlockingQueue<Integer> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);
  List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>();
  Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
    try {
      for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) queue.put(i);
      queue.put(-1); // poison pill: no more work
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
      Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
    }
  });
  producer.start();
  for (int x; (x = queue.take()) != -1; ) out.add(x);
  producer.join();
  return out; // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
}
Complexity: O(1) per put and take, plus waiting

