Concept: Sentinel head and tail nodes remove every special case from a doubly linked list
Misconception: Unlinking a node from a doubly linked list only needs prev.next to change.
Explanation: Each node has prev and next, so unlinking must fix two neighbours: n.prev.next = n.next and n.next.prev = n.prev. Forgetting either leaves the list half-connected. Two sentinel nodes that are never removed (head and tail) guarantee every real node has both neighbours, so insert and unlink need no null checks. Clearing the unlinked node's own pointers avoids stale references.
Incorrect:
void unlink(Node n) { n.prev.next = n.next; } // n.next.prev still points at n
Correct:
void unlink(Node n) {
  n.prev.next = n.next;
  n.next.prev = n.prev;
  n.prev = n.next = null;
}
Complexity: O(1) per insert and unlink

