Java · grid · patterns · L4
Concept: Seed every source before the BFS loop, then count levels
Misconception: Spreading from several starting cells needs one BFS per source.
Explanation: When several cells spread at once (rotting oranges, distance to the nearest gate), offer all of them to one ArrayDeque before the loop. The BFS then expands from every source in parallel, so the first time a cell is reached is its shortest distance to any source. Process the queue level by level with a size snapshot to count minutes or steps, and mark cells as they are offered.
Incorrect:
for (int[] src : rottenCells) bfsFrom(src); // repeats work and overcounts minutes
Correct:
static int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
  int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length, fresh = 0, minutes = 0;
  Deque<int[]> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
  for (int r = 0; r < m; r++)
    for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
      if (grid[r][c] == 2) queue.offer(new int[] {r, c});
      else if (grid[r][c] == 1) fresh++;
    }
  int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
  while (!queue.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {
    for (int size = queue.size(); size > 0; size--) {
      int[] cell = queue.poll();
      for (int[] d : dirs) {
        int r = cell[0] + d[0], c = cell[1] + d[1];
        if (r >= 0 && r < m && c >= 0 && c < n && grid[r][c] == 1) {
          grid[r][c] = 2;
          fresh--;
          queue.offer(new int[] {r, c});
        }
      }
    }
    minutes++;
  }
  return fresh == 0 ? minutes : -1;
}
Complexity: O(m · n)

