Java · binary_tree · transformation · L4
Concept: Build a tree from preorder and inorder with a HashMap of inorder positions
Misconception: Finding the root's position in the inorder array with a linear search is fast enough.
Explanation: The next preorder value is the current subtree's root, and its position in the inorder array splits the subtree into left and right parts. Searching for that position each time costs O(n) per node, O(n^2) overall on skewed trees. Store each value's inorder index in a HashMap once, and keep the preorder cursor in a field, since a primitive parameter would be copied per call. The method requires unique values.
Incorrect:
int mid = indexOf(inorder, rootVal, lo, hi); // O(n) scan for every node
Correct:
class BuildTree {
  private int pre;
  private int[] preorder;
  private final Map<Integer, Integer> inIndex = new HashMap<>();
  TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
    this.preorder = preorder;
    pre = 0;
    inIndex.clear();
    for (int i = 0; i < inorder.length; i++) inIndex.put(inorder[i], i);
    return build(0, inorder.length - 1);
  }
  private TreeNode build(int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo > hi) return null;
    TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[pre++]);
    int mid = inIndex.get(root.val);
    root.left = build(lo, mid - 1);
    root.right = build(mid + 1, hi);
    return root;
  }
}
Complexity: O(n) time and space

