Java · heap · insertion-and-deletion · L4
Concept: Mark removals in a map and discard them when they reach the top of the heap
Misconception: PriorityQueue.remove(x) is a cheap way to delete arbitrary elements.
Explanation: PriorityQueue.remove(Object) searches the whole array, O(n) per call. Lazy deletion records pending removals in a HashMap of counts instead, and only discards an element when it surfaces at the top. Keep a separate size counter, because the heap still physically holds the removed elements until they surface. Sliding-window statistics use this pattern.
Incorrect:
heap.remove(outgoing); // O(n) every time the window slides
Correct:
final class LazyHeap {
  private final PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
  private final Map<Integer, Integer> removed = new HashMap<>();
  private int size;
  void add(int x) { heap.offer(x); size++; }
  void remove(int x) { removed.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum); size--; } // x must be present
  int peek() { prune(); return heap.peek(); } // call only when size() > 0
  int size() { return size; }
  private void prune() {
    while (!heap.isEmpty() && removed.getOrDefault(heap.peek(), 0) > 0) {
      removed.merge(heap.poll(), -1, Integer::sum);
    }
  }
}
Complexity: Amortised O(log n) per operation

