Java · trie · patterns · L6
Concept: A binary trie over bits finds the maximum XOR pair by choosing the opposite bit greedily
Misconception: The maximum XOR of two numbers requires comparing every pair.
Explanation: Insert each non-negative int into a trie by its bits from 30 down to 0, so each node has two children. To maximise x XOR y, walk the trie for x and prefer, at every bit, the child with the opposite bit; when it exists, that bit of the result is 1. High bits are decided first, which is why the greedy choice is optimal. Storing children in an int[][] with node indices avoids allocating a node object per bit.
Incorrect:
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) best = Math.max(best, nums[i] ^ nums[j]); // O(n^2)
Correct:
static int findMaximumXOR(int[] nums) {
  int[][] next = new int[nums.length * 31 + 1][2]; // node -> child for bit 0 and bit 1
  int count = 1, best = 0;
  for (int x : nums) {
    int node = 0;
    for (int b = 30; b >= 0; b--) {
      int bit = (x >> b) & 1;
      if (next[node][bit] == 0) next[node][bit] = count++;
      node = next[node][bit];
    }
    node = 0;
    int xor = 0;
    for (int b = 30; b >= 0; b--) {
      int bit = (x >> b) & 1;
      if (next[node][bit ^ 1] != 0) { xor |= 1 << b; node = next[node][bit ^ 1]; }
      else node = next[node][bit];
    }
    best = Math.max(best, xor);
  }
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(31 · n) time and space

