Java · graph · edge-cases · L4
Concept: Never add an edge weight to an unreached Integer.MAX_VALUE distance
Misconception: Integer.MAX_VALUE behaves like infinity in distance updates.
Explanation: Shortest-path code marks unreached nodes with Integer.MAX_VALUE. Adding any positive weight to it wraps to a large negative number, which then wins every comparison and corrupts every later distance. Relax only from nodes whose distance is finite, as Dijkstra does by relaxing from polled nodes, and as Bellman-Ford must check explicitly; or store distances in long.
Incorrect:
if (prev[u] + w < dist[v]) dist[v] = prev[u] + w; // overflows when prev[u] is MAX_VALUE
Correct:
static int[] bellmanFord(int n, int[][] edges, int src) {
  int[] dist = new int[n];
  Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
  dist[src] = 0;
  for (int round = 1; round < n; round++) {
    int[] prev = dist.clone();
    for (int[] e : edges) {
      int u = e[0], v = e[1], w = e[2];
      if (prev[u] != Integer.MAX_VALUE && prev[u] + w < dist[v]) dist[v] = prev[u] + w;
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
Complexity: O(V · E) for Bellman-Ford

