Java · union_find · mutation · L6
Concept: Undoable union-find uses union by size without path compression, plus a history stack
Misconception: Any union-find can undo its last union.
Explanation: Path compression rewrites parent links during find, so those changes cannot be undone cheaply. A rollback union-find skips compression and relies on union by size alone, which keeps trees O(log n) deep. Each successful union pushes the attached root and its new parent onto a stack; undo pops it, restores the root's parent and subtracts the size. Offline algorithms use this to add and remove edges in a controlled order.
Incorrect:
int find(int x) { /* with path compression */ } // undo cannot restore the rewritten links
Correct:
final class RollbackUnionFind {
  private final int[] parent, size;
  private final Deque<int[]> history = new ArrayDeque<>(); // {attached root, new parent}
  RollbackUnionFind(int n) {
    parent = new int[n];
    size = new int[n];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { parent[i] = i; size[i] = 1; }
  }
  int find(int x) { while (parent[x] != x) x = parent[x]; return x; }
  boolean union(int a, int b) {
    int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
    if (ra == rb) return false;
    if (size[ra] < size[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }
    parent[rb] = ra;
    size[ra] += size[rb];
    history.push(new int[] {rb, ra});
    return true;
  }
  void undo() {
    int[] last = history.pop();
    parent[last[0]] = last[0];
    size[last[1]] -= size[last[0]];
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n) per find and union, O(1) per undo

