Java · map · ordering · L4
Concept: TreeMap answers nearest-key questions in O(log n)
Misconception: Finding the largest key at or below a value requires scanning all keys.
Explanation: TreeMap keeps keys sorted in a balanced tree. floorKey(x) returns the largest key <= x, ceilingKey(x) the smallest key >= x, lowerKey and higherKey the strict versions; each returns null when no such key exists, so do not unbox the result into an int without checking. floorEntry and ceilingEntry return the key and value together. This turns timeline lookups, interval bookings and sweep lines into a few lines of code.
Incorrect:
int best = -1;
for (int t : history.keySet()) if (t <= time) best = Math.max(best, t); // O(n) per query
Correct:
static String latestAtOrBefore(TreeMap<Integer, String> history, int time) {
  Map.Entry<Integer, String> e = history.floorEntry(time);
  return e == null ? "" : e.getValue();
}
Complexity: O(log n) per query

