Java · queue · iteration · L2
Concept: Read queue.size() once per level, before adding children
Misconception: for (int i = 0; i < queue.size(); i++) processes exactly one level of a BFS.
Explanation: The loop condition is re-evaluated every iteration, and the queue's size changes as nodes are polled and their children offered, so the loop runs the wrong number of times and mixes levels. Read the size once into a variable before the loop: for (int size = queue.size(); size > 0; size--). Children added during the loop then wait for the next level.
Incorrect:
