Java · queue · complexity · L1
Concept: ArrayList.remove(0) is O(n); ArrayDeque.poll is O(1)
Misconception: Any list works as a BFS queue if items are removed from the front.
Explanation: ArrayList stores elements contiguously, so removing index 0 shifts every remaining element left: O(n) per removal and O(n^2) for a BFS over n nodes. This is the Java version of JavaScript's shift problem. ArrayDeque is a circular array whose head simply moves, so offer and poll are O(1) amortised. LinkedList is also O(1) but allocates a node per element and exposes List methods such as remove(int) that are easy to call by mistake.
Incorrect:
List<Integer> queue = new ArrayList<>(List.of(start));
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
  int u = queue.remove(0); // shifts the whole list every time
}
Correct:
static int countReachable(List<List<Integer>> adj, int start) {
  boolean[] seen = new boolean[adj.size()];
  Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
  queue.offer(start);
  seen[start] = true;
  int count = 0;
  while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int u = queue.poll();
    count++;
    for (int v : adj.get(u)) if (!seen[v]) { seen[v] = true; queue.offer(v); }
  }
  return count;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) with ArrayDeque, O(V^2 + E) with ArrayList.remove(0)

