Java · deque · patterns · L4
Concept: Keep window candidates in a deque of indices with decreasing values
Misconception: The maximum of each sliding window has to be found by scanning the window.
Explanation: An element can never be a window's maximum once a larger element appears after it, so it can be discarded from the back. An element also stops mattering once its index leaves the window, so it is discarded from the front. A Deque<Integer> of indices with values decreasing from front to back keeps the current maximum at the front. Each index is offered once and polled at most once, so the whole pass is O(n).
Incorrect:
for (int i = 0; i + k <= nums.length; i++) {
  int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
  for (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) max = Math.max(max, nums[j]); // O(n * k)
}
Correct:
static int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
  int[] res = new int[nums.length - k + 1];
  Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();
  for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    if (!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() <= i - k) dq.pollFirst();
    while (!dq.isEmpty() && nums[dq.peekLast()] <= nums[i]) dq.pollLast();
    dq.offerLast(i);
    if (i >= k - 1) res[i - k + 1] = nums[dq.peekFirst()];
  }
  return res;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(k) space

