Concept: Fenwick trees are 1-indexed; updates climb with i += i & -i and queries descend with i -= i & -i
Misconception: A Fenwick tree can be indexed from 0 like any other array.
Explanation: Each tree[i] stores the sum of the block (i - lowbit(i), i], where lowbit(i) = i & -i. At i = 0 the lowbit is 0, so a loop starting there never advances and the program hangs. Store the tree 1-indexed in a long[n + 1] and add 1 to caller indices at the boundary. Updates climb to every block that contains i; prefix queries descend through disjoint blocks that together cover [1, i]. Set operations must add the difference from the current value.
Incorrect:
void add(int i, long delta) {
  for (; i < tree.length; i += i & -i) tree[i] += delta; // hangs when i == 0
}
Correct:
void add(int i, long delta) {
  for (i++; i < tree.length; i += i & -i) tree[i] += delta;
}
long prefix(int i) {
  long s = 0;
  for (i++; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += tree[i];
  return s;
}
Complexity: O(log n) per add and prefix

