Java · Java Segment Trees and Fenwick Trees · From prefix sums to Fenwick trees
The canonical class hides the 1-indexing from its callers.

```java
final class Fenwick {
    private final long[] tree; // 1-indexed: tree[i] holds the sum of (i - lowbit(i), i]

    Fenwick(int n) {
        tree = new long[n + 1];
    }

    void add(int i, long delta) { // i is 0-indexed for callers
        for (i++; i < tree.length; i += i & -i) tree[i] += delta;
    }

    long prefix(int i) { // sum of [0, i], 0-indexed; prefix(-1) is 0
        long s = 0;
        for (i++; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += tree[i];
        return s;
    }

    long rangeSum(int l, int r) { // inclusive [l, r]
        return prefix(r) - prefix(l - 1);
    }
}

// Range Sum Query - Mutable: keep the current values to turn "set" into "add a difference".
class NumArray {
    private final int[] values;
    private final Fenwick fenwick;

    NumArray(int[] nums) {
        values = nums.clone();
        fenwick = new Fenwick(nums.length);
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) fenwick.add(i, nums[i]);
    }

    void update(int index, int val) {
        fenwick.add(index, (long) val - values[index]);
        values[index] = val;
    }

    int sumRange(int left, int right) {
        return (int) fenwick.rangeSum(left, right);
    }
}
```

Two arrows summarise the class: updates climb (`i += i & -i`) and queries descend (`i -= i & -i`). Learners who remember that pair can rebuild the rest.

