Three snippets cover the patterns.

```java
// 1. Non-mutating set algebra: copy, then apply the mutating operation.
static <T> Set<T> union(Set<T> a, Set<T> b) {
    Set<T> s = new HashSet<>(a);
    s.addAll(b);
    return s;
}

static <T> Set<T> intersection(Set<T> a, Set<T> b) {
    Set<T> small = a.size() <= b.size() ? a : b;
    Set<T> large = small == a ? b : a;
    Set<T> s = new HashSet<>(small); // copy the smaller set, so retainAll does less work
    s.retainAll(large);
    return s;
}

// 2. TreeSet nearest-value query: is any of the last k values within t of nums[i]?
static boolean nearbyAlmostDuplicate(int[] nums, int k, int t) {
    TreeSet<Long> window = new TreeSet<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        Long ceil = window.ceiling((long) nums[i] - t); // smallest value >= nums[i] - t
        if (ceil != null && ceil <= (long) nums[i] + t) return true;
        window.add((long) nums[i]);
        if (i >= k) window.remove((long) nums[i - k]); // long, to match the element type
    }
    return false;
}
```

