Java · stack · complexity · L4
Concept: Deep recursion throws StackOverflowError; an explicit ArrayDeque removes the limit
Misconception: A recursive solution that passes small tests works for any input size.
Explanation: Each recursive call uses a frame on the thread's stack, which is limited, and Java performs no tail-call optimisation. A skewed tree or a long path of tens of thousands of nodes throws StackOverflowError, an Error that should not be caught. Pushing pending work onto an ArrayDeque gives the same traversal with no depth limit. Running recursion on a thread with a larger requested stack is a workaround only, because the size is a hint the JVM may ignore.
Incorrect:
static int maxDepth(TreeNode n) {
  return n == null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(n.left), maxDepth(n.right));
} // StackOverflowError on a 100,000-node chain
Correct:
static int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
  if (root == null) return 0;
  Deque<Object[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
  stack.push(new Object[] {root, 1});
  int best = 0;
  while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
    Object[] top = stack.pop();
    TreeNode node = (TreeNode) top[0];
    int depth = (int) top[1];
    best = Math.max(best, depth);
    if (node.left != null) stack.push(new Object[] {node.left, depth + 1});
    if (node.right != null) stack.push(new Object[] {node.right, depth + 1});
  }
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) heap space instead of call-stack space

