Java · Java Stacks · End operations track
Two snippets cover the representations learners should use: `ArrayDeque` for clarity, an array for speed.

```java
// 1. ArrayDeque as a stack: one set of methods, all at the head.
Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
stack.push('(');
char top = stack.peek(); // '(' (peek returns null when empty)
char removed = stack.pop(); // throws NoSuchElementException when empty
boolean empty = stack.isEmpty();

// 2. A primitive int stack: no boxing, no null, fixed capacity.
final class IntStack {
    private int[] items;
    private int top = 0; // number of items; the top is items[top - 1]

    IntStack(int capacity) {
        items = new int[capacity];
    }

    void push(int x) {
        if (top == items.length) items = Arrays.copyOf(items, items.length * 2); // grow
        items[top++] = x;
    }

    int pop() {
        if (top == 0) throw new IllegalStateException("pop from an empty stack");
        return items[--top];
    }

    int peek() {
        if (top == 0) throw new IllegalStateException("peek at an empty stack");
        return items[top - 1];
    }

    boolean isEmpty() {
        return top == 0;
    }
}
```

In interviews `ArrayDeque` is expected and clearer; the `IntStack` is worth knowing for monotonic-stack problems over large arrays, where boxing dominates the running time.

