```java
// 3. Explicit-stack in-order traversal with early exit: k-th smallest in a BST.
static int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
    Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    TreeNode node = root;
    while (node != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
        while (node != null) {
            stack.push(node);
            node = node.left;
        }
        node = stack.pop();
        if (--k == 0) return node.val;
        node = node.right;
    }
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("k is larger than the tree");
}

// 4. Last resort for deep recursion: run it on a thread with a larger stack.
int[] result = new int[1];
Thread worker = new Thread(null, () -> result[0] = maxDepth(root), "deep", 1L << 28); // 256 MB requested
worker.start();
worker.join(); // join throws InterruptedException; handle or declare it
```

Snippet 4 is a workaround, not a fix: the requested stack size is only a hint to the JVM, and some judges ignore it. Converting the recursion to an explicit stack, as in snippet 3, is the reliable answer.

