Java · string · mutation · L2
Concept: Build strings in loops with one StringBuilder
Misconception: result += c inside a loop is as cheap as appending to a list.
Explanation: Strings are immutable, so result += c creates a new String and copies every character built so far. Over n iterations that is O(n^2) work. A StringBuilder appends into one growing buffer in amortised O(1) per character; call toString() once at the end. Its equals method compares references, so compare builders through toString() or compareTo.
Incorrect:
String result = "";
for (char c : s.toCharArray()) if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(c)) result += Character.toLowerCase(c);
Correct:
static String clean(String s) {
  StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(s.length());
  for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
    if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(c)) sb.append(Character.toLowerCase(c));
  }
  return sb.toString();
}
Complexity: O(n) with StringBuilder vs O(n^2) with +=

