Java · binary_tree · edge-cases · L3
Concept: Pass long bounds down a BST check so extreme int values stay valid
Misconception: Integer.MIN_VALUE and Integer.MAX_VALUE are safe "no bound" values for Validate BST.
Explanation: The BST rule must hold for whole subtrees, so a correct check passes an open interval (low, high) down each path. If the initial bounds are Integer.MIN_VALUE and Integer.MAX_VALUE, a node whose value equals one of them fails the strict comparison, so the single-node tree [2147483647] is wrongly reported invalid. Use long bounds starting at Long.MIN_VALUE and Long.MAX_VALUE, or nullable Integer bounds where null means unbounded.
Incorrect:
static boolean valid(TreeNode n, int low, int high) {
  if (n == null) return true;
  if (n.val <= low || n.val >= high) return false;
  return valid(n.left, low, n.val) && valid(n.right, n.val, high);
} // valid(root, Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE) rejects [2147483647]
Correct:
static boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
  return valid(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
}
static boolean valid(TreeNode n, long low, long high) {
  if (n == null) return true;
  if (n.val <= low || n.val >= high) return false;
  return valid(n.left, low, n.val) && valid(n.right, n.val, high);
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(h) stack

