Java · binary_tree · patterns · L2
Concept: Record new ArrayList<>(path), and remove the last element when backtracking
Misconception: result.add(path) saves the current path.
Explanation: Java passes a copy of the List reference by value. These recursive calls refer to the same mutable path object; rebinding a parameter would not rebind the caller's variable. Adding it to the result stores a reference to that same object, which keeps changing and is usually empty when the search ends. Copy it with new ArrayList<>(path) at the moment it is recorded, and undo each choice after the recursive calls with path.remove(path.size() - 1), where remove(int) removing by index is exactly what is wanted.
Incorrect:
if (node.left == null && node.right == null && remaining == node.val) result.add(path);
Correct:
static List<List<Integer>> pathSum(TreeNode root, int target) {
  List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
  dfs(root, target, new ArrayList<>(), result);
  return result;
}
static void dfs(TreeNode node, int remaining, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> result) {
  if (node == null) return;
  path.add(node.val);
  if (node.left == null && node.right == null && remaining == node.val) {
    result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
  }
  dfs(node.left, remaining - node.val, path, result);
  dfs(node.right, remaining - node.val, path, result);
  path.remove(path.size() - 1); // undo the choice
}
Complexity: O(n · h) time including copies, O(h) for the path

