Java · binary_tree · transformation · L4
Concept: Write null markers to the output, but queue only real nodes
Misconception: Level-order serialisation can queue null children like any other node.
Explanation: Serialisation must record missing children, or different trees produce the same string. ArrayDeque, the usual Java queue, throws NullPointerException when offered null. Append a marker such as # to the StringBuilder for each missing child and queue only real nodes. When deserialising, handle the empty string first, because "".split(",") returns one empty token, and check for the marker before calling Integer.parseInt.
Incorrect:
queue.offer(node.left);  // NullPointerException when node.left is null
queue.offer(node.right);
Correct:
static String serialize(TreeNode root) {
  if (root == null) return "";
  StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder().append(root.val);
  Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
  queue.offer(root);
  while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    TreeNode node = queue.poll();
    for (TreeNode child : new TreeNode[] {node.left, node.right}) {
      if (child == null) sb.append(",#");
      else { sb.append(',').append(child.val); queue.offer(child); }
    }
  }
  return sb.toString();
}
Complexity: O(n) time and space

