static int height(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) return 0;
    int l = height(node.left);
    if (l == -1) return -1;
    int r = height(node.right);
    if (r == -1 || Math.abs(l - r) > 1) return -1;
    return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
}

// 4. The library's balanced BST: ordered queries without writing a tree.
TreeSet<Integer> values = new TreeSet<>(List.of(20, 5, 15, 10));
Integer justBelow = values.floor(12);   // 10
Integer justAbove = values.ceiling(12); // 15
Integer smallest = values.first();      // 5
```

Snippet 4 is the bridge from interview trees to production Java: when a real program needs a sorted, changing collection, `TreeMap` or `TreeSet` is the answer, not a hand-written BST.

