Java · trie · creation · L1
Concept: Node[26] children are fast and alphabetical, but accept only lowercase a-z
Misconception: A Node[26] trie works for any word, as long as the index is computed with c - 'a'.
Explanation: An array of 26 child references indexed by c - 'a' is the idiomatic Java trie for lowercase input: O(1) child lookup, and iterating indices 0 to 25 visits children in alphabetical order. Every other character produces an index outside 0..25 and throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException ('A' - 'a' is -32). New arrays start as 26 nulls, so check before descending. When input is not guaranteed lowercase, normalise it or use Map<Character, Node> children (TreeMap for sorted output).
