Java · trie · iteration · L3
Concept: Reuse one StringBuilder; append before each child and setLength after
Misconception: Passing path + ch to each recursive call is an efficient way to build words in a trie DFS.
Explanation: Strings are immutable, so path + ch copies the whole path at every node visited, which adds O(L) work and an allocation per node. A single StringBuilder can be shared by the whole search: append the character before descending into a child, and call setLength(length - 1) after returning to undo it. Call toString() only when a word is recorded. With Node[26] children, looping c from 0 to 25 returns words alphabetically.
Incorrect:
collect(node.next[c], path + (char) ('a' + c), out); // a new String at every node
Correct:
static void collect(Node node, StringBuilder path, List<String> out, int limit) {
  if (out.size() >= limit) return;
  if (node.end) out.add(path.toString());
  for (int c = 0; c < 26; c++) {
    if (node.next[c] == null) continue;
    path.append((char) ('a' + c));
    collect(node.next[c], path, out, limit);
    path.setLength(path.length() - 1);
  }
}
Complexity: O(nodes visited) plus O(L) per recorded word

