Java · trie · patterns · L4
Concept: Store words on end nodes, mark char cells with '#', restore them, and prune
Misconception: Word Search II is Word Search run once per dictionary word.
Explanation: Build a trie of the words and store each word on its end node. Run one DFS from every cell that walks the char[][] board and the trie together: mark the cell with '#' before exploring and restore the original char afterwards. Report a word when its node is reached, then set node.word = null so it is reported once. After exploring a node, remove it from its parent if it has no word and no children, so later searches skip finished branches.
Incorrect:
for (String w : words) if (exist(board, w)) found.add(w); // one full board search per word
Correct:
private void dfs(char[][] board, int r, int c, Node parent, List<String> found) {
  if (r < 0 || r >= board.length || c < 0 || c >= board[0].length) return;
  char ch = board[r][c];
  if (ch == '#') return;
  Node node = parent.next[ch - 'a'];
  if (node == null) return;
  if (node.word != null) { found.add(node.word); node.word = null; }
  board[r][c] = '#';
  dfs(board, r + 1, c, node, found);
  dfs(board, r - 1, c, node, found);
  dfs(board, r, c + 1, node, found);
  dfs(board, r, c - 1, node, found);
  board[r][c] = ch;
  if (node.word == null && isLeaf(node)) parent.next[ch - 'a'] = null;
}
Complexity: O(m · n · 4 · 3^(L-1)) worst case; pruning removes most of it in practice

