Java · union_find · mutation · L0
Concept: A group is identified by its root; union and connectivity both work on roots
Misconception: parent[a] = b merges a's and b's groups, and parent[a] == parent[b] means they are connected.
Explanation: Union-find stores each group as a tree in an int[] parent, and only the root (the node that is its own parent) identifies the group. Setting parent[a] = b moves only a, leaving the rest of a's group behind. Two members of one group can have different direct parents, so comparing parent[a] with parent[b] gives false negatives. Always call find on both sides, link one root under the other, and compare roots.
Incorrect:
void union(int a, int b) { parent[a] = b; }
boolean connected(int a, int b) { return parent[a] == parent[b]; }
Correct:
boolean union(int a, int b) {
  int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
  if (ra == rb) return false;
  parent[ra] = rb;
  return true;
}
boolean connected(int a, int b) { return find(a) == find(b); }
Complexity: O(depth) per find without optimisations

