Java · union_find · complexity · L1
Concept: Path compression plus union by size make union-find nearly O(1) per operation
Misconception: Union-find is fast with or without its optimisations.
Explanation: Without optimisations, unions can build a chain, and find walks the whole chain: O(n) per call. Union by size attaches the smaller tree under the larger, which keeps trees shallow. Path compression makes every node visited by find point to the root (or, with halving, to its grandparent), so later finds take one step. Together they give an amortised cost bounded by the inverse Ackermann function, which is at most 4 for any realistic input. Write find iteratively so very large inputs cannot overflow the stack.
Incorrect:
int find(int x) { return parent[x] == x ? x : find(parent[x]); } // no compression, recursive
Correct:
int find(int x) {
  int root = x;
  while (parent[root] != root) root = parent[root];
  while (parent[x] != root) { int next = parent[x]; parent[x] = root; x = next; }
  return root;
}
boolean union(int a, int b) {
  int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
  if (ra == rb) return false;
  if (size[ra] < size[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }
  parent[rb] = ra;
  size[ra] += size[rb];
  return true;
}
Complexity: Nearly O(1) amortised per operation

