Java · Java Union-Find (Disjoint Set) · Core operations track
The canonical class uses full compression and union by size; path halving (one pass, shown in the graph blueprint) gives the same complexity.

```java
final class UnionFind {
    private final int[] parent, size;
    private int count; // number of groups

    UnionFind(int n) {
        parent = new int[n];
        size = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            parent[i] = i;
            size[i] = 1;
        }
        count = n;
    }

    int find(int x) {
        int root = x;
        while (parent[root] != root) root = parent[root];
        while (parent[x] != root) { // full path compression: point every visited node at the root
            int next = parent[x];
            parent[x] = root;
            x = next;
        }
        return root;
    }

    boolean union(int a, int b) { // false when a and b were already in one group
        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) return false;
        if (size[ra] < size[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }
        parent[rb] = ra; // smaller tree under the larger
        size[ra] += size[rb];
        count--;
        return true;
    }

    boolean connected(int a, int b) { return find(a) == find(b); }

    int count() { return count; }

    int sizeOf(int x) { return size[find(x)]; }
}
```

`sizeOf` reads the size from the root, because only roots keep accurate sizes; this is what problems such as "largest group" or Number of Islands II need.

