Concept: === compares arrays by reference, so compare contents element by element and copy before storing
Misconception: [1, 2] === [1, 2] is true, and pushing path into the results saves its current contents.
Explanation: Arrays are objects, so ===, includes and indexOf compare references, never contents. Compare two arrays by length and then element by element. The same rule causes the classic backtracking bug: res.push(path) stores a reference to the one path array, which is empty again by the end, so push a copy with [...path]. Spread and slice copy one level; structuredClone copies nested arrays too.
Incorrect:
