JavaScript · JavaScript Arrays · Index handling track
These four snippets are the canonical answers learners should be able to write from memory.

```javascript
// 1. Remove every x in place: loop backwards so splice never skips an element.
for (let i = a.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (a[i] === x) a.splice(i, 1);

// 2. Same result in O(n): compact with a write pointer, then truncate.
let w = 0;
for (let r = 0; r < a.length; r++) if (a[r] !== x) a[w++] = a[r];
a.length = w;

// 3. Safe circular index, even for negative i.
const wrap = (i, n) => ((i % n) + n) % n;

// 4. Rotate right by k in place with three reversals.
function rotate(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  k = ((k % n) + n) % n;
  const rev = (l, r) => { for (; l < r; l++, r--) [a[l], a[r]] = [a[r], a[l]]; };
  rev(0, n - 1);
  rev(0, k - 1);
  rev(k, n - 1);
}
```

The drill format that builds this skill fastest is the trace table: the learner writes `i`, the relevant values and the array after every step of a short loop, then the chatbot runs the same code and compares.

