JavaScript · graph · creation · L1
Concept: An undirected edge must be stored in both nodes' neighbour lists
Misconception: Storing each edge once, as given in the input, is enough for an undirected graph.
Explanation: In an undirected graph an edge [u, v] lets a traversal move from u to v and from v to u. If it is stored only in u's list, a traversal starting at v never reaches u, so component counts come out too high and reachability checks fail. Add the edge to both lists. Also create each node's list separately: Array(n).fill([]) shares one list between every node.
Incorrect:
const adj = Array(n).fill([]);          // one shared list
for (const [u, v] of edges) adj[u].push(v); // one direction only
Correct:
function buildUndirected(n, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    adj[v].push(u);
  }
  return adj;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time and space

