JavaScript · graph · patterns · L2
Concept: Detect a directed cycle by tracking which nodes are on the current path
Misconception: In a directed graph, reaching an already-visited node during DFS means there is a cycle.
Explanation: In a directed graph two paths can lead to the same node without forming a cycle, as in a diamond: 0 -> 1 -> 3 and 0 -> 2 -> 3. Reaching node 3 a second time is harmless because it is already finished. A cycle exists only when DFS reaches a node that is still on the current path. Track three states: 0 unvisited, 1 on the current path, 2 finished.
Incorrect:
const visited = new Set();
const dfs = u => {
  if (visited.has(u)) return true; // reports the diamond as a cycle
  visited.add(u);
  return adj[u].some(dfs);
};
Correct:
function hasCycle(n, adj) {
  const state = new Array(n).fill(0);
  const dfs = u => {
    if (state[u] === 1) return true;  // back on the current path: a cycle
    if (state[u] === 2) return false; // finished earlier: no cycle through here
    state[u] = 1;
    for (const v of adj[u]) if (dfs(v)) return true;
    state[u] = 2;
    return false;
  };
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (dfs(i)) return true;
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

