JavaScript · graph · patterns · L2
Concept: Remove nodes with in-degree 0 to get an order, and a cycle check for free
Misconception: Kahn's algorithm always returns a complete order.
Explanation: Kahn's algorithm counts incoming edges for every node, queues the nodes with none, and repeatedly removes a queued node and decrements its neighbours' counts, queueing any that reach zero. The removal order is a valid topological order. Nodes on a cycle never reach zero, so if fewer than n nodes are removed the graph has a cycle. Build edges in the direction "must come first -> comes after"; for Course Schedule's [a, b] that is b -> a.
Incorrect:
// ... same loop ...
return order; // a partial order is returned for cyclic input
Correct:
function topoOrder(n, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indegree = new Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indegree[v]++; }
  const queue = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indegree[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
  const order = [];
  for (let head = 0; head < queue.length; head++) {
    const u = queue[head];
    order.push(u);
    for (const v of adj[u]) if (--indegree[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
  }
  return order.length === n ? order : [];
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time and space

