JavaScript · graph · patterns · L3
Concept: Union-find answers "are these connected?" as edges arrive
Misconception: Merging two groups means setting one node's parent to the other node.
Explanation: Union-find keeps each group as a tree identified by its root. Merging must connect the roots, not the nodes themselves, or groups are linked incorrectly. Path halving during find and attaching the smaller tree under the larger keep every operation nearly O(1). union returns false when both nodes already share a root, which means the new edge closes a cycle; counting successful unions gives the number of groups.
Incorrect:
function union(a, b) {
  parent[a] = b; // links nodes, not roots
}
Correct:
class UnionFind {
  constructor(n) {
    this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
    this.size = new Array(n).fill(1);
    this.count = n;
  }
  find(x) {
    while (this.parent[x] !== x) {
      this.parent[x] = this.parent[this.parent[x]];
      x = this.parent[x];
    }
    return x;
  }
  union(a, b) {
    let ra = this.find(a), rb = this.find(b);
    if (ra === rb) return false;
    if (this.size[ra] < this.size[rb]) [ra, rb] = [rb, ra];
    this.parent[rb] = ra;
    this.size[ra] += this.size[rb];
    this.count--;
    return true;
  }
}
Complexity: Nearly O(1) amortised per operation (inverse Ackermann), O(n) space

