JavaScript · grid · complexity · L4
Concept: Flood fill with an explicit stack, because recursion overflows on large regions
Misconception: A recursive DFS flood fill works on any grid that fits in memory.
Explanation: A recursive flood fill keeps one call-stack frame per cell on its current path. A large or snake-shaped region makes that path tens of thousands of cells long, past the roughly ten thousand frames Node allows, and the fill throws RangeError. An array used as a stack has no such limit. Mark each cell when it is pushed, so no cell is pushed twice.
Incorrect:
function sink(g, r, c) {
  if (r < 0 || r >= g.length || c < 0 || c >= g[0].length || g[r][c] !== '1') return;
  g[r][c] = '0';
  sink(g, r + 1, c); sink(g, r - 1, c); sink(g, r, c + 1); sink(g, r, c - 1);
}
Correct:
function sinkIsland(grid, sr, sc) {
  const rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;
  const stack = [[sr, sc]];
  grid[sr][sc] = '0';
  let size = 0;
  while (stack.length) {
    const [r, c] = stack.pop();
    size++;
    for (const [dr, dc] of [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]]) {
      const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;
      if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && grid[nr][nc] === '1') {
        grid[nr][nc] = '0'; // mark when pushed
        stack.push([nr, nc]);
      }
    }
  }
  return size;
}
Complexity: O(rows * cols) time and heap space in the worst case

