JavaScript · grid · transformation · L3
Concept: Rotate a square matrix clockwise in place by transposing it and then reversing each row
Misconception: Rotating in place means writing each cell straight to its new position.
Explanation: Writing matrix[c][n - 1 - r] = matrix[r][c] overwrites a value before it has moved. Split the rotation into two moves that are each safe in place. Transposing swaps [r][c] with [c][r] for c > r only; looping over every pair swaps each one twice and changes nothing. Reversing every row then completes a clockwise turn. For anticlockwise, reverse the rows first and then transpose.
Incorrect:
for (let r = 0; r < n; r++)
  for (let c = 0; c < n; c++) matrix[c][n - 1 - r] = matrix[r][c]; // overwrites unmoved cells
Correct:
function rotate(matrix) {
  const n = matrix.length;
  for (let r = 0; r < n; r++) {
    for (let c = r + 1; c < n; c++) {
      [matrix[r][c], matrix[c][r]] = [matrix[c][r], matrix[r][c]];
    }
  }
  for (const row of matrix) row.reverse();
}
Complexity: O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space

