JavaScript · heap · patterns · L4
Concept: Dijkstra pushes duplicates and skips the stale ones
Misconception: When a shorter distance to a node is found, its old entry must be found and updated inside the heap.
Explanation: JavaScript heaps have no decrease-key. Instead, push a new [distance, node] entry whenever a shorter distance is found, and when an entry is popped, skip it if its distance is larger than the best distance recorded for that node. The first pop of each node is its final shortest distance, provided every edge weight is non-negative.
Incorrect:
const [d, u] = pq.pop();
for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) { /* relaxes from stale, longer distances too */ }
Correct:
function dijkstra(n, adj, source) {
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  dist[source] = 0;
  const pq = new Heap((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  pq.push([0, source]);
  while (pq.size) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (d + w < dist[v]) { dist[v] = d + w; pq.push([dist[v], v]); }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
Complexity: O((V + E) log E) time, O(V + E) space

