Concept: Fast and slow pointers detect a cycle; compare the nodes with ===, not their values
Misconception: A list has a cycle when a value appears twice.
Explanation: Values can repeat in a list with no cycle. A cycle means some node's next leads back to a node already passed. Move slow one step and fast two steps at a time; with a cycle, fast eventually lands on the same node object as slow, which === detects. If fast reaches null, there is no cycle. This uses O(1) memory, where a Set of visited nodes uses O(n).
Incorrect:
function hasCycle(head) {
  const seenValues = new Set();
  for (let n = head; n; n = n.next) {
    if (seenValues.has(n.val)) return true; // a repeated value is not a cycle
    seenValues.add(n.val);
  }
  return false;
}
Correct:
function hasCycle(head) {
  let slow = head, fast = head;
  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

