JavaScript · array · mutation · L2
Concept: Compact an array in place with a read index and a write index
Misconception: Removing elements in place requires calling splice inside the loop.
Explanation: Walk the array once with a read index. Copy each element you keep to arr[write] and advance write. At the end, truncate with arr.length = write. Every element moves at most once, so the pass is O(n) instead of the O(n^2) of repeated splice calls, and the kept elements stay in their original order.
Incorrect:
const nums = [0, 1, 0, 3, 12];
for (let i = nums.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (nums[i] === 0) nums.splice(i, 1); // O(n^2)
Correct:
function removeValue(nums, val) {
  let write = 0;
  for (let read = 0; read < nums.length; read++) {
    if (nums[read] !== val) nums[write++] = nums[read];
  }
  nums.length = write;
  return write;
}
removeValue([0, 1, 0, 3, 12], 0); // returns 3; the array becomes [1, 3, 12]
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) extra space

