JavaScript · map · ordering · L2
Concept: A Map iterates in insertion order, and delete followed by set moves a key to the end
Misconception: Calling set on an existing key moves it to the end of the iteration order.
Explanation: set on a key that already exists keeps its original position. To mark a key as most recently used, delete it and set it again. The first key, map.keys().next().value, is then the least recently used one, so a Map on its own implements an LRU cache with O(1) get and put. Java needs a LinkedHashMap in access order for the same trick.
