JavaScript · set · mental-model · L0
Concept: A Set stores unique values with O(1) add, has and delete, unlike Array.includes
Misconception: Checking an array with includes is as fast as checking a Set with has.
Explanation: arr.includes(x) scans the array, so calling it once per element makes a loop O(n^2). A Set keeps one copy of each value and answers has in O(1) on average. Primitives compare by value, with NaN equal to NaN, while objects and arrays compare by reference. add returns the set, and size counts the values.
Incorrect:
function hasDuplicate(nums) {
  const seen = [];
  for (const x of nums) { if (seen.includes(x)) return true; seen.push(x); } // O(n^2)
  return false;
}
Correct:
function hasDuplicate(nums) {
  const seen = new Set();
  for (const x of nums) {
    if (seen.has(x)) return true;
    seen.add(x);
  }
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(1) average per operation; O(n) for the whole loop

