Concept: Dequeue by advancing a head index, because shift is O(n)
Misconception: queue.shift() is O(1) because arrays work as queues.
Explanation: Arrays have no O(1) way to remove the first element. shift moves every remaining element, so a BFS over n nodes that uses shift can take O(n^2). Keep a head index instead: read queue[head++], and treat the queue as empty when head equals queue.length. The array is freed when the BFS ends; a long-running queue should compact itself or use a ring buffer.
Incorrect:
const queue = [start];
while (queue.length) {
  const node = queue.shift(); // O(n) per dequeue
}
Correct:
function bfsOrder(adj, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const seen = new Set([start]);
  for (let head = 0; head < queue.length; head++) {
    const node = queue[head];
    for (const next of adj[node]) {
      if (!seen.has(next)) { seen.add(next); queue.push(next); }
    }
  }
  return queue; // the nodes in visit order
}
Complexity: O(1) per enqueue and dequeue

