JavaScript · queue · patterns · L3
Concept: Put every source in the queue at distance 0, then run one BFS
Misconception: To find each cell's distance to its nearest source, run a separate BFS from each source.
Explanation: One BFS per source costs O(sources * cells). Put all the sources in the queue at the start, at distance 0, and run a single BFS. The first time a cell is reached, it is reached from its nearest source. Rotting Oranges counts the levels this way, and Walls and Gates writes each distance into the grid.
Incorrect:
for (const [r, c] of sources) bfsFrom(r, c); // O(sources * cells)
Correct:
function orangesRotting(grid) {
  const rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length;
  const queue = [];
  let fresh = 0;
  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
      if (grid[r][c] === 2) queue.push([r, c]);
      else if (grid[r][c] === 1) fresh++;
    }
  }
  const dirs = [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]];
  let minutes = 0, head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length && fresh > 0) {
    const levelEnd = queue.length;
    for (; head < levelEnd; head++) {
      const [r, c] = queue[head];
      for (const [dr, dc] of dirs) {
        const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;
        if (nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols && grid[nr][nc] === 1) {
          grid[nr][nc] = 2;
          fresh--;
          queue.push([nr, nc]);
        }
      }
    }
    minutes++;
  }
  return fresh === 0 ? minutes : -1;
}
Complexity: O(rows * cols) time and space

