JavaScript · binary_tree · ordering · L3
Concept: An inorder traversal of a BST visits values in ascending order, so it can stop early
Misconception: Finding the k-th smallest value in a BST means collecting every value and sorting.
Explanation: Inorder visits the left subtree, then the node, then the right subtree, which in a BST is ascending order. Counting during the traversal finds the k-th smallest value in O(h + k) and stops as soon as it gets there. The same property validates a BST, since each value must be larger than the one visited before it.
Incorrect:
const vals = [];
collect(root, vals);
vals.sort((a, b) => a - b); // O(n log n), and it visits every node
const kth = vals[k - 1];
Correct:
function kthSmallest(root, k) {
  const stack = [];
  let node = root;
  while (node || stack.length) {
    while (node) { stack.push(node); node = node.left; }
    node = stack.pop();
    if (--k === 0) return node.val;
    node = node.right;
  }
}
Complexity: O(h + k) time, O(h) space

