JavaScript · heap · insertion-and-deletion · L1
Concept: push sifts the new last element up, and pop moves the last element to the root and sifts it down
Misconception: After the top is removed, the smaller child simply moves up to replace it.
Explanation: Promoting children leaves a hole that breaks the complete shape. Instead, pop swaps the root with the last element, removes it, and sifts the new root down, swapping it with its smaller child until neither child is smaller. push appends at the end and swaps with the parent while the new value is smaller. Each operation follows one path from the root to a leaf, so it is O(log n).
Incorrect:
// Removing the top with shift is O(n), and the array is no longer a heap.
const top = heap.shift();
Correct:
function siftUp(h, i) {
  while (i > 0) {
    const p = (i - 1) >> 1;
    if (h[p] <= h[i]) break;
    [h[p], h[i]] = [h[i], h[p]];
    i = p;
  }
}
function siftDown(h, i) {
  for (;;) {
    const l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1;
    let m = i;
    if (l < h.length && h[l] < h[m]) m = l;
    if (r < h.length && h[r] < h[m]) m = r;
    if (m === i) return;
    [h[m], h[i]] = [h[i], h[m]];
    i = m;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n) per push or pop

