JavaScript · heap · creation · L2
Concept: JavaScript has no built-in priority queue, so write one small Heap class that takes a comparator
Misconception: Sorting the array after every push is a good enough priority queue.
Explanation: JavaScript has no built-in heap, so solutions either write one or use one the platform provides; LeetCode's JavaScript runtime includes @datastructures-js/priority-queue. The other heap and graph chunks use this API: new Heap(compare), where compare(a, b) below 0 means a comes out first, plus push, pop, peek and a size getter. Sorting after every push costs O(n log n) per push instead of O(log n).
Incorrect:
const pq = [];
function push(x) { pq.push(x); pq.sort((a, b) => a - b); } // O(n log n) per push
Correct:
class Heap {
  #a = [];
  #cmp;
  constructor(cmp = (x, y) => x - y) { this.#cmp = cmp; }
  get size() { return this.#a.length; }
  peek() { return this.#a[0]; }
  push(x) {
    const a = this.#a;
    a.push(x);
    let i = a.length - 1;
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = (i - 1) >> 1;
      if (this.#cmp(a[p], a[i]) <= 0) break;
      [a[p], a[i]] = [a[i], a[p]];
      i = p;
    }
  }
  pop() {
    const a = this.#a, top = a[0], last = a.pop();
    if (a.length) {
      a[0] = last;
      let i = 0;
      for (;;) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1;
        let m = i;
        if (l < a.length && this.#cmp(a[l], a[m]) < 0) m = l;
        if (r < a.length && this.#cmp(a[r], a[m]) < 0) m = r;
        if (m === i) break;
        [a[m], a[i]] = [a[i], a[m]];
        i = m;
      }
    }
    return top;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n) for push and pop, O(1) for peek and size

