JavaScript · heap · mutation · L5
Concept: An indexed heap supports decrease-key by tracking where each item sits in the array
Misconception: Lazy deletion is always good enough, so a heap never needs decrease-key.
Explanation: With lazy deletion the heap can grow to O(E) entries. When it must hold one entry per item, for example on a dense graph or in a scheduler that changes priorities often, keep a Map from each item to its array index and update it on every swap. After a priority changes, sift that index up or down. Each update is O(log n), and the heap never holds more than n entries.
Incorrect:
// Linear search for the item, then a full rebuild: O(n) per update.
const i = heap.findIndex(e => e.id === id);
heap[i].pri = newPri;
heapify(heap);
Correct:
class IndexedMinHeap {
  a = [];
  pos = new Map(); // item id -> index in a
  #swap(i, j) {
    const a = this.a;
    [a[i], a[j]] = [a[j], a[i]];
    this.pos.set(a[i].id, i);
    this.pos.set(a[j].id, j);
  }
  #up(i) {
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = (i - 1) >> 1;
      if (this.a[p].pri <= this.a[i].pri) break;
      this.#swap(i, p);
      i = p;
    }
  }
  #down(i) {
    for (;;) {
      const l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1;
      let m = i;
      if (l < this.a.length && this.a[l].pri < this.a[m].pri) m = l;
      if (r < this.a.length && this.a[r].pri < this.a[m].pri) m = r;
      if (m === i) return;
      this.#swap(i, m);
      i = m;
    }
  }
  push(id, pri) { this.a.push({ id, pri }); this.pos.set(id, this.a.length - 1); this.#up(this.a.length - 1); }
  decreaseKey(id, pri) { const i = this.pos.get(id); this.a[i].pri = pri; this.#up(i); }
  pop() {
    const top = this.a[0];
    this.#swap(0, this.a.length - 1);
    this.a.pop();
    this.pos.delete(top.id);
    if (this.a.length) this.#down(0);
    return top;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n) per push, pop and decreaseKey; O(n) space

