JavaScript · trie · mental-model · L0
Concept: A trie stores strings one character per edge, so words that share a prefix share nodes
Misconception: A trie stores whole words inside its nodes.
Explanation: Each node holds a map from the next character to a child node. Inserting a word walks from the root one character at a time and creates the children that are missing. All words with the same prefix pass through the same node, so checking a prefix of length m costs O(m) however many words are stored. A flag on a node marks where a word ends.
Incorrect:
const words = ['car', 'card', 'care'];
const hasPrefix = p => words.some(w => w.startsWith(p)); // scans every word on each query
Correct:
class TrieNode {
  children = new Map();
  isEnd = false;
}
function insert(root, word) {
  let node = root;
  for (const ch of word) {
    if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
    node = node.children.get(ch);
  }
  node.isEnd = true;
}
Complexity: O(m) per insert or lookup for a word of length m

