JavaScript · trie · patterns · L3
Concept: For autocomplete, walk to the prefix node, then DFS in sorted child order and stop after k words
Misconception: Autocomplete must collect every word under the prefix and then sort them.
Explanation: After walking the prefix, only that node's subtree matters. A DFS that visits children in alphabetical order produces words in lexicographic order, so it can stop as soon as it has k words. When there are many queries, storing the top suggestions on each node at insert time makes each query O(prefix length). Search Suggestions System asks for three words per typed prefix.
Incorrect:
// Collects every word below the prefix, then sorts them all, on every query.
const all = [];
collectAll(prefixNode, prefix, all);
return all.sort().slice(0, 3);
Correct:
function suggestions(root, prefix, k = 3) {
  let node = root;
  for (const ch of prefix) {
    node = node.children.get(ch);
    if (!node) return [];
  }
  const out = [];
  (function dfs(n, path) {
    if (n.isEnd) out.push(path);
    for (const ch of [...n.children.keys()].sort()) {
      if (out.length === k) return;
      dfs(n.children.get(ch), path + ch);
    }
  })(node, prefix);
  return out.slice(0, k);
}
Complexity: O(prefix length + nodes visited before k words are found)

