Concept: Iterative DFS uses an array as a stack and marks each node as seen when it is pushed
Misconception: A DFS loop with a stack needs no visited set, because each node is popped only once.
Explanation: Without a visited set, a cycle pushes the same nodes forever, and shared neighbours are pushed many times. Mark a node as seen when it is pushed, or skip seen nodes when they are popped, and use one rule consistently. An explicit stack avoids stack overflow on deep graphs such as a chain of 100,000 nodes. It suits reachability and components, but post-order work such as cycle detection needs extra state.
Incorrect:
const stack = [start];
while (stack.length) {
  const u = stack.pop();
  for (const v of adj[u]) stack.push(v); // no visited set: loops forever on a cycle
}
Correct:
function reachable(adj, start) {
  const seen = new Set([start]);
  const stack = [start];
  while (stack.length) {
    const u = stack.pop();
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (!seen.has(v)) { seen.add(v); stack.push(v); }
    }
  }
  return seen;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

