Concept: Kruskal sorts edges by weight and keeps each edge that joins two different components
Misconception: Taking every node's cheapest edge always produces a minimum spanning tree.
Explanation: Each node's cheapest edge can leave the graph in several pieces. Kruskal sorts all the edges by weight and keeps each one whose ends are in different components, which union-find answers in nearly O(1). It stops after n - 1 edges. For dense inputs given as points, such as Min Cost to Connect All Points, Prim's algorithm with an O(n^2) loop avoids building all n^2 edges.
Incorrect:
// Keeps each node's cheapest edge: can leave the graph in several pieces.
const chosen = adj.map(list => list.reduce((best, e) => (e[1] < best[1] ? e : best)));
Correct:
function kruskal(n, edges) { // edges are [u, v, weight]
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
  const find = x => {
    while (parent[x] !== x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
    return x;
  };
  let total = 0, used = 0;
  for (const [u, v, w] of [...edges].sort((a, b) => a[2] - b[2])) {
    const ru = find(u), rv = find(v);
    if (ru === rv) continue;
    parent[ru] = rv;
    total += w;
    if (++used === n - 1) break;
  }
  return used === n - 1 ? total : -1; // -1 means the graph is disconnected
}
Complexity: O(E log E) time, O(V) space

