JavaScript · map · mutation · L1
Concept: Group values by creating each group's array once and pushing into it
Misconception: m.set(key, [...(m.get(key) ?? []), value]) is a fine way to add a value to a group.
Explanation: Spreading the old group into a new array copies the whole group on every append, so a group of k items costs O(k^2) to build. Create the array once when the key is new, then push into it. When every item is available up front, Map.groupBy (ES2024, Node 21 and later) builds the whole grouping in one call.
Incorrect:
const groups = new Map();
for (const w of ['eat', 'tea']) {
  const key = [...w].sort().join('');
  groups.set(key, [...(groups.get(key) ?? []), w]); // copies the group each time
}
Correct:
function groupAnagrams(strs) {
  const groups = new Map();
  for (const s of strs) {
    const key = [...s].sort().join('');
    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);
    groups.get(key).push(s);
  }
  return [...groups.values()];
}
Complexity: O(n * k log k) for Group Anagrams with sorted keys of length k

