JavaScript · map · ordering · L4
Concept: JavaScript has no TreeMap, so keep keys in a sorted array and binary search it for floor and ceiling queries
Misconception: A Map can answer "largest key at or below t" because it keeps its keys in order.
Explanation: A Map keeps insertion order, not sorted order, and has no floor or ceiling lookup. When keys arrive in increasing order, as timestamps do in Time Based Key-Value Store, append them to an array and binary search it in O(log n). When they arrive in any order, keep the array sorted with a binary-search insert, which costs O(n) per insert, or use a balanced tree library. Java solves the same problem with TreeMap.floorEntry.
Incorrect:
let best = '';
for (const [t, v] of history) if (t <= time) best = v; // O(n) per query
Correct:
class TimeMap {
  #m = new Map(); // key -> [[timestamp, value], ...] in increasing timestamp order
  set(key, value, t) {
    if (!this.#m.has(key)) this.#m.set(key, []);
    this.#m.get(key).push([t, value]);
  }
  get(key, t) {
    const list = this.#m.get(key) ?? [];
    let lo = 0, hi = list.length - 1, ans = '';
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
      if (list[mid][0] <= t) { ans = list[mid][1]; lo = mid + 1; } else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return ans;
  }
}
Complexity: O(1) amortised per set and O(log n) per get

