JavaScript · JavaScript Maps · Order and iteration track
The LRU cache is the canonical exercise for this track: the Map's own order is the recency list, so no linked list is needed.

```javascript
class LRUCache {
  #capacity;
  #map = new Map();

  constructor(capacity) {
    this.#capacity = capacity;
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.#map.has(key)) return -1;
    const value = this.#map.get(key);
    this.#map.delete(key); // move to the newest position
    this.#map.set(key, value);
    return value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    this.#map.delete(key);
    this.#map.set(key, value);
    if (this.#map.size > this.#capacity) {
      this.#map.delete(this.#map.keys().next().value); // evict the oldest entry
    }
  }
}

// The trap from the table: re-adding the current key during iteration never ends.
// for (const [k, v] of m) { m.delete(k); m.set(k, v); }
```

LRU Cache is a NeetCode 150 problem rather than a Blind 75 one, but it is the best single test of this track and worth adding to the corpus.

