JavaScript · JavaScript Objects · Object model track
Two snippets make the track concrete: one security bug and one design-class bug.

```javascript
// 1. Prototype pollution through a naive deep merge.
function merge(target, src) {
  for (const k in src) {
    if (typeof src[k] === 'object' && src[k] !== null) merge(target[k] ??= {}, src[k]);
    else target[k] = src[k];
  }
  return target;
}
merge({}, JSON.parse('{"__proto__": {"isAdmin": true}}'));
({}).isAdmin; // true: every object now inherits it
// Fix: skip unsafe keys inside the loop.
const UNSAFE = new Set(['__proto__', 'constructor', 'prototype']);

// 2. A design-class method loses `this` when passed as a callback.
class MedianFinder {
  #values = [];
  addNum(n) { this.#values.push(n); }
}
const finder = new MedianFinder();
[1, 2, 3].forEach(n => finder.addNum(n)); // works
[1, 2, 3].forEach(finder.addNum);         // TypeError: `this` is undefined inside addNum
```

The prototype-pollution snippet doubles as a security lesson worth having in a portfolio product: it shows learners why "just merge the JSON" is not safe on a server.

