Concept: Processing BFS one level at a time
Misconception: Reading the queue's current length inside the loop gives the size of the current level.
Explanation: Children are added to the back while the current level is being processed, so the queue's length keeps growing. Record where the level ends before processing it, and stop there; everything added after that point belongs to the next level.
Incorrect:
while (head < queue.length) {
  const level = [];
  for (; head < queue.length; head++) {   // keeps running into the next level
    const node = queue[head];
    level.push(node.val);
    if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);
    if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);
  }
  levels.push(level);
}
Correct:
while (head < queue.length) {
  const levelEnd = queue.length;            // snapshot first
  const level = [];
  while (head < levelEnd) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    level.push(node.val);
    if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);
    if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);
  }
  levels.push(level);
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space for the queue array

