JavaScript · queue · patterns · L2
Concept: BFS reaches every node first along a shortest path when each edge costs 1
Misconception: A DFS that explores every path finds shortest paths just as well as BFS.
Explanation: BFS leaves nodes in order of their distance from the start, so the first time a node is queued, its distance is final: record dist[v] = dist[u] + 1 at that moment. DFS finds a path, not necessarily the shortest, and exploring every path to compare them is exponential. With weighted edges, BFS no longer works and Dijkstra's algorithm is needed.
Incorrect:
function dfsDistance(adj, u, t, seen = new Set()) {
  if (u === t) return 0;
  seen.add(u);
  for (const v of adj[u]) if (!seen.has(v)) {
    const d = dfsDistance(adj, v, t, seen);
    if (d >= 0) return d + 1; // the first path found, not the shortest
  }
  return -1;
}
Correct:
function shortestPath(adj, s, t) {
  const dist = new Array(adj.length).fill(-1);
  dist[s] = 0;
  const queue = [s];
  for (let head = 0; head < queue.length; head++) {
    const u = queue[head];
    if (u === t) return dist[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[v] === -1) { dist[v] = dist[u] + 1; queue.push(v); }
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

