JavaScript · queue · creation · L3
Concept: A fixed-size circular array gives O(1) enqueue and dequeue and reuses its slots
Misconception: A head index into a growing array is a good queue for a long-running stream.
Explanation: With a head index, the slots before head are never reused, so a queue that runs forever keeps growing even when it holds few items. A ring buffer stores items in a fixed array and wraps both ends with modulo: the tail slot is (head + size) % capacity. Track size separately, because head === tail happens both when the buffer is empty and when it is full.
Incorrect:
const queue = [];
let head = 0;
function onEvent(x) { queue.push(x); if (queue.length - head > 100) head++; } // the array never shrinks
Correct:
class CircularQueue {
  #buf; #head = 0; #size = 0;
  constructor(k) { this.#buf = new Array(k); }
  enQueue(x) {
    if (this.#size === this.#buf.length) return false;
    this.#buf[(this.#head + this.#size) % this.#buf.length] = x;
    this.#size++;
    return true;
  }
  deQueue() {
    if (this.#size === 0) return false;
    this.#head = (this.#head + 1) % this.#buf.length;
    this.#size--;
    return true;
  }
  Front() { return this.#size ? this.#buf[this.#head] : -1; }
  Rear() { return this.#size ? this.#buf[(this.#head + this.#size - 1) % this.#buf.length] : -1; }
}
Complexity: O(1) per operation, O(k) space

