JavaScript · deque · patterns · L4
Concept: Keep a deque of indexes with decreasing values, so the window maximum is always at the front
Misconception: The maximum of each sliding window has to be found by scanning the window.
Explanation: Once a larger value arrives, smaller values before it can never be a window maximum, so drop them from the back. Once an index leaves the window, drop it from the front. An array with a head index works as the deque: advance head for the front, pop for the back. Each index enters and leaves once, so the pass is O(n) instead of O(n * k).
Incorrect:
const out = [];
for (let i = 0; i + k <= nums.length; i++) out.push(Math.max(...nums.slice(i, i + k))); // O(n * k)
Correct:
function maxSlidingWindow(nums, k) {
  const dq = []; // indexes; their values decrease from dq[head] to the end
  let head = 0;
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    if (head < dq.length && dq[head] <= i - k) head++;
    while (dq.length > head && nums[dq.at(-1)] <= nums[i]) dq.pop();
    dq.push(i);
    if (i >= k - 1) out.push(nums[dq[head]]);
  }
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space for the index array

