JavaScript · set · patterns · L2
Concept: Mark a node visited when it is queued, not when it is processed
Misconception: It does not matter whether a node is marked visited before or after it leaves the queue.
Explanation: If marking waits until a node is dequeued, every neighbour that sees it before then queues it again. The answer may still be right, but the queue grows and work repeats, and counting problems can double-count. Mark on enqueue so each node enters the queue exactly once.
Incorrect:
const [r, c] = queue[h];
visited.add(cell(r, c));             // too late
for (const [nr, nc] of neighbours(r, c))
  if (!visited.has(cell(nr, nc))) queue.push([nr, nc]);
Correct:
const [r, c] = queue[h];
for (const [nr, nc] of neighbours(r, c)) {
  const k = cell(nr, nc);
  if (!visited.has(k)) { visited.add(k); queue.push([nr, nc]); }
}
Complexity: O(V + E) with each node queued once

