JavaScript · set · access · L1
Concept: Set.prototype.add returns the set, not a boolean, so it cannot test for a duplicate
Misconception: if (!seen.add(x)) detects a duplicate, as it does in Java.
Explanation: Java's Set.add returns false when the element was already present. JavaScript's add returns the Set itself, which is always truthy, so that condition never fires and duplicates pass silently. Check has before add, or compare size before and after the add. delete, by contrast, does return a boolean.
Incorrect:
function hasDuplicate(nums) {
  const seen = new Set();
  for (const x of nums) if (!seen.add(x)) return true; // never true
  return false;
}
Correct:
function hasDuplicate(nums) {
  const seen = new Set();
  for (const x of nums) {
    const before = seen.size;
    seen.add(x);
    if (seen.size === before) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(1) average per add; O(n) for the loop

