JavaScript · set · ordering · L2
Concept: A Set iterates in insertion order, not sorted order, so use has to find neighbours instead of sorting
Misconception: A Set keeps its values sorted, like Java's TreeSet.
Explanation: Sets and Maps iterate in the order values were first added, and JavaScript has no built-in sorted set. When sorted output is needed, spread and sort with a numeric comparator in O(n log n). Often order is not needed at all: Longest Consecutive Sequence finds each run's start with has(x - 1) and counts upwards with has, which is O(n) overall.
Incorrect:
const set = new Set([3, 1, 2]);
const smallest = [...set][0]; // 3: the first value added, not the smallest
Correct:
function longestConsecutive(nums) {
  const set = new Set(nums);
  let best = 0;
  for (const x of set) {
    if (set.has(x - 1)) continue; // x is not the start of a run
    let len = 1;
    while (set.has(x + len)) len++;
    best = Math.max(best, len);
  }
  return best;
}
const sorted = [...new Set([3, 1, 2])].sort((a, b) => a - b); // [1, 2, 3]
Complexity: O(n) for the has-based scan; O(n log n) to sort

