JavaScript · set · edge-cases · L4
Concept: A Number bitmask has 32 usable bits; the sign bit requires careful interpretation and shift-based subset loop bounds have a narrower domain
Misconception: 1 << 32 sets bit 32 of a mask.
Explanation: Bit i of an integer can record whether i is in a set: add with mask | (1 << i) and test with (mask >> i) & 1. JavaScript converts both operands to 32-bit signed integers and takes the shift count modulo 32, so 1 << 31 is negative and 1 << 32 is 1. Bit 31 is usable as a flag even though the Number result is signed; test membership with a nonzero bitwise result, not a positive-result check. The subset loop below requires 0 <= nums.length <= 30 because its bound uses a signed left shift; practical enumeration requires much smaller inputs. For wider sets, use BigInt (1n << 40n) or an array of booleans. Enumerating all masks from 0 to 2^n - 1 lists every subset.
Incorrect:
let mask = 0;
mask |= 1 << 32;              // sets bit 0, not bit 32
const negative = 1 << 31;     // -2147483648
Correct:
function subsets(nums) {
  if (nums.length > 30) throw new RangeError("Signed shift loop requires at most 30 elements");
  const out = [];
  for (let mask = 0; mask < 1 << nums.length; mask++) {
    out.push(nums.filter((_, i) => (mask >> i) & 1));
  }
  return out;
}
const wide = (1n << 40n) | 1n;          // a 41-bit set as a BigInt
const has40 = ((wide >> 40n) & 1n) === 1n; // true
Complexity: O(1) for fixed-width Number bit operations; BigInt costs grow with operand width. Enumerating all subsets costs O(2^n * n) time and output space.

