JavaScript · JavaScript Sets · Membership and identity track
These five snippets are the canonical answers learners should write from memory.

```javascript
// 1. Deduplicate while keeping the order of first appearance.
const unique = [...new Set(arr)];

// 2. Deduplicate objects by a key, keeping the first occurrence.
const seenIds = new Set();
const firstById = items.filter(x => !seenIds.has(x.id) && seenIds.add(x.id)); // add returns the Set, which is truthy

// 3. Detect a duplicate in one call: add never fails, so compare sizes.
const before = seen.size;
seen.add(x);
const isNew = seen.size > before;

// 4. BFS on a grid: mark a cell when it is queued, not when it is dequeued.
const cell = (r, c) => `${r},${c}`;
const visited = new Set([cell(sr, sc)]);
const queue = [[sr, sc]];
for (let h = 0; h < queue.length; h++) {
  const [r, c] = queue[h];
  for (const [dr, dc] of [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]]) {
    const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc, k = cell(nr, nc);
    if (inBounds(nr, nc) && !visited.has(k)) { visited.add(k); queue.push([nr, nc]); }
  }
}

// 5. Count a run only from its first value (Longest Consecutive Sequence).
const values = new Set(nums);
let best = 0;
for (const n of values) {
  if (values.has(n - 1)) continue;
  let len = 1;
  while (values.has(n + len)) len++;
  best = Math.max(best, len);
}
```

Snippet 5 is the Blind 75 reference solution for Longest Consecutive Sequence; asking why it stays O(n) despite the inner `while` checks whether the learner understands the "start only once" idea.

