JavaScript · stack · transformation · L4
Concept: Replacing recursion with an explicit stack
Misconception: Recursion is free, and any recursive solution works for any input size.
Explanation: Every recursive call adds a frame to the call stack, which engines cap and V8 never optimises away for tail calls. Deep inputs throw RangeError. Pushing the pending work onto an explicit array stack gives the same visiting order with no depth limit, and makes early exit a plain return.
Incorrect:
const maxDepth = node => node ? 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(node.left), maxDepth(node.right)) : 0;
// a 100,000-node chain throws RangeError
Correct:
function maxDepth(root) {
  if (!root) return 0;
  const stack = [[root, 1]];
  let best = 0;
  while (stack.length) {
    const [node, d] = stack.pop();
    best = Math.max(best, d);
    if (node.left) stack.push([node.left, d + 1]);
    if (node.right) stack.push([node.right, d + 1]);
  }
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(n) time; O(n) heap memory for the explicit stack, with no call-stack limit

