Concept: Store the running minimum beside each item so getMin stays O(1) after pops
Misconception: A single variable holding the minimum is enough for a stack with getMin.
Explanation: After the minimum is popped, one variable cannot recover the previous minimum without scanning the whole stack. Keep a second stack in which entry i is the minimum of the first i + 1 items. push stores min(x, previous minimum), and pop removes from both, so push, pop, top and getMin are all O(1). Private # fields keep outside code from breaking the pairing.
Incorrect:
class MinStack {
  items = []; min = Infinity;
  push(x) { this.items.push(x); this.min = Math.min(this.min, x); }
  pop() { this.items.pop(); } // min is now stale if the minimum was popped
}
Correct:
class MinStack {
  #items = [];
  #mins = []; // #mins[i] is the minimum of #items[0..i]
  push(x) {
    this.#items.push(x);
    this.#mins.push(this.#mins.length ? Math.min(x, this.#mins.at(-1)) : x);
  }
  pop() { this.#mins.pop(); return this.#items.pop(); }
  top() { return this.#items.at(-1); }
  getMin() { return this.#mins.at(-1); }
}
Complexity: O(1) per operation, O(n) extra space

