JavaScript · stack · edge-cases · L2
Concept: In postfix evaluation the right operand pops first, and division truncates toward zero
Misconception: The first value popped is the left operand.
Explanation: The right operand was pushed last, so it comes off the stack first: pop b, then pop a, then compute a op b. Addition and multiplication hide the mistake; subtraction and division expose it. Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation truncates division toward zero, so use Math.trunc(a / b). Math.floor rounds negative results down and gives the wrong answer.
Incorrect:
const a = stack.pop(), b = stack.pop();
stack.push(a - b); // ["4", "1", "-"] gives -3 instead of 3
Correct:
function evalRPN(tokens) {
  const ops = {
    '+': (a, b) => a + b,
    '-': (a, b) => a - b,
    '*': (a, b) => a * b,
    '/': (a, b) => Math.trunc(a / b),
  };
  const stack = [];
  for (const t of tokens) {
    if (Object.hasOwn(ops, t)) {
      const b = stack.pop(), a = stack.pop();
      stack.push(ops[t](a, b));
    } else {
      stack.push(Number(t));
    }
  }
  return stack.pop();
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

