JavaScript · JavaScript Stacks · End operations track
A small wrapper class makes these rules explicit and is itself a good first exercise, since it forces decisions about empty pops and iteration order.

```javascript
class Stack {
  #items = [];

  push(x) {
    this.#items.push(x);
    return this; // chainable, unlike Array.prototype.push
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.#items.length === 0) throw new RangeError('pop from an empty stack');
    return this.#items.pop();
  }

  peek() {
    return this.#items.at(-1); // undefined when empty
  }

  get size() {
    return this.#items.length;
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.#items.length === 0;
  }

  *[Symbol.iterator]() { // top to bottom, matching pop order
    for (let i = this.#items.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) yield this.#items[i];
  }
}
```

In interviews a plain array is expected and faster to write; the wrapper earns its place in production code and as a teaching tool for the empty-stack decision.

