```javascript
// 4. Queue from two stacks: each item moves at most once, so operations are amortised O(1).
class StackQueue {
  #in = [];
  #out = [];
  push(x) { this.#in.push(x); }
  pop() { this.#refill(); return this.#out.pop(); }
  peek() { this.#refill(); return this.#out.at(-1); }
  empty() { return this.#in.length === 0 && this.#out.length === 0; }
  #refill() { if (!this.#out.length) while (this.#in.length) this.#out.push(this.#in.pop()); }
}
```

The call stack is the hidden stack behind every recursive solution in the corpus. JavaScript engines cap it (typically around ten thousand frames, varying with frame size), V8 does not perform tail-call optimisation, and exceeding the cap throws `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`. Converting recursion to an explicit stack, snippet 3, is the fix learners should be able to apply on demand.

