Concept: Edit characters in an array and build strings from parts with one join
Misconception: s = s.slice(0, i) + c + s.slice(i + 1) is a cheap way to change one character.
Explanation: Strings are immutable, so that line copies the whole string: O(n) per edit and O(n^2) across a loop. Convert to an array once, edit cells in O(1), and join once at the end. Appending with += is fast in modern engines, but an array of parts with push, pop and join is the clearest fit for backtracking, where pop undoes the last choice.
Incorrect:
let s = 'hello';
for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) s = s.slice(0, i) + s[i].toUpperCase() + s.slice(i + 1); // O(n^2)
Correct:
const chars = [...'hello'];
chars[0] = 'j';
const word = chars.join(''); // 'jello'
function generateParenthesis(n) {
  const out = [], path = [];
  (function go(open, close) {
    if (path.length === 2 * n) { out.push(path.join('')); return; }
    if (open < n) { path.push('('); go(open + 1, close); path.pop(); }
    if (close < open) { path.push(')'); go(open, close + 1); path.pop(); }
  })(0, 0);
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(1) per array edit and O(n) per join, instead of O(n) per string edit

