JavaScript · binary_tree · patterns · L1
Concept: Every recursive tree function has a three-part contract
Misconception: A recursive tree function only has to visit every node; what it returns can be worked out later.
Explanation: A recursive function on a binary tree must decide three things before any code is written: what it returns for null (the answer for an empty tree), what it passes down to each child, and what it returns up to its parent. Most binary-tree bugs break one of these. Writing the contract as a one-line comment first makes the base case and the use of the children's results explicit.
Incorrect:
function countNodes(node) {
  if (!node) return 0;
  countNodes(node.left);
  countNodes(node.right);
  return 1; // the children's answers are thrown away
}
Correct:
// Contract: returns the number of nodes in this subtree; null returns 0.
function countNodes(node) {
  if (!node) return 0;
  return 1 + countNodes(node.left) + countNodes(node.right);
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(h) stack space

