Concept: A BST check must pass value bounds down the whole path
Misconception: A binary tree is a valid BST if every node is larger than its left child and smaller than its right child.
Explanation: The BST rule applies to whole subtrees, not just children: every value in a node's right subtree must be larger than the node, including grandchildren. Checking only children accepts [5, 4, 6, null, null, 3, 7], where 3 sits in 5's right subtree. Pass an open interval (low, high) down the recursion: going left narrows high to the node's value, going right narrows low.
Incorrect:
function isValidBST(node) {
  if (!node) return true;
  if (node.left && node.left.val >= node.val) return false;
  if (node.right && node.right.val <= node.val) return false;
  return isValidBST(node.left) && isValidBST(node.right);
}
Correct:
function isValidBST(node, low = -Infinity, high = Infinity) {
  if (!node) return true;
  if (node.val <= low || node.val >= high) return false;
  return isValidBST(node.left, low, node.val) && isValidBST(node.right, node.val, high);
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(h) stack space

