JavaScript · binary_tree · patterns · L5
Concept: Return one branch upward, record the two-branch path globally
Misconception: The function can return the best path through a node, including both children, and an empty path (sum 0) is a valid answer.
Explanation: A path can bend at most once. At each node, the best path that bends there is node.val plus the best non-negative gain from each side; record it in a global maximum. The parent can only extend one side, so return node.val plus the larger gain. Negative gains are dropped with Math.max(0, ...). The global maximum starts at -Infinity because the path must contain at least one node, so an all-negative tree returns its largest single value.
Incorrect:
let best = 0;                                  // wrong for all-negative trees
const gain = n => {
  if (!n) return 0;
  const total = n.val + gain(n.left) + gain(n.right);
  best = Math.max(best, total);
  return total;                                // a parent cannot use both branches
};
Correct:
function maxPathSum(root) {
  let best = -Infinity;
  const gain = node => {
    if (!node) return 0;
    const left = Math.max(0, gain(node.left));
    const right = Math.max(0, gain(node.right));
    best = Math.max(best, node.val + left + right); // path that bends here
    return node.val + Math.max(left, right);         // path that continues upward
  };
  gain(root);
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(h) stack space

