JavaScript · trie · mental-model · L1
Concept: A path is a prefix; only an end marker makes it a word
Misconception: If the walk for a string reaches a node, the string is a stored word.
Explanation: In a trie every node on a path represents a prefix, and inserting "car" creates nodes for "c", "ca" and "car". Only the last node gets an end marker. search must therefore walk the path and check the marker, while startsWith only needs the path to exist. With only "car" stored, search('ca') is false and startsWith('ca') is true.
Incorrect:
search(word) {
  return this.find(word) !== null; // true for every stored prefix
}
Correct:
search(word) {
  return this.find(word)?.end === true;
}
startsWith(prefix) {
  return this.find(prefix) !== null;
}
Complexity: O(L) per call, where L is the string length

