JavaScript · trie · creation · L2
Concept: Keep children in a Map, separate from the node's flags
Misconception: A plain object can hold both a node's children and its flags, keyed by character.
Explanation: A plain object inherits keys such as constructor, so a trie over words or path segments can find segments that were never inserted. Mixing flags like end into the same object also breaks as soon as a segment is called "end". Give each node a children Map and separate fields for its flags. Use Array(26) only when every character is guaranteed to be a lowercase letter; it is fast and keeps alphabetical order, but wastes memory on sparse tries.
Incorrect:
const root = {};
function insertPath(path) {
  let node = root;
  for (const seg of path.split('/')) node = node[seg] ??= {}; // 'constructor' resolves to Object
  node.end = true;                                             // collides with a segment named 'end'
}
Correct:
const makeNode = () => ({ children: new Map(), end: false });
const root = makeNode();
function insertPath(path) {
  let node = root;
  for (const seg of path.split('/')) {
    if (!node.children.has(seg)) node.children.set(seg, makeNode());
    node = node.children.get(seg);
  }
  node.end = true;
}
Complexity: O(segments) per insert; one Map per node

