JavaScript · trie · patterns · L3
Concept: A wildcard branches into every child; a letter follows one
Misconception: At a wildcard, returning the result of the first child's search is enough.
Explanation: When the pattern has '.', any child may lead to a match, so the search must try each child and succeed if any of them succeeds. Returning inside the loop stops after the first child even when it fails. Letters follow exactly one child, or fail if it is missing. At the end of the pattern the current node must be an end node. A pattern of only dots can visit every node down to its length, which is the worst case.
Incorrect:
if (ch === '.') {
  for (const child of node.children.values()) return matches(child, word, i + 1);
  return false;
}
Correct:
function matches(node, word, i = 0) {
  if (i === word.length) return node.end;
  const ch = word[i];
  if (ch === '.') {
    for (const child of node.children.values()) {
      if (matches(child, word, i + 1)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
  const child = node.children.get(ch);
  return child ? matches(child, word, i + 1) : false;
}
Complexity: O(L) with no wildcards; up to O(nodes) when the pattern is mostly wildcards

