JavaScript · trie · patterns · L4
Concept: Search the board once while walking a trie, and prune finished branches
Misconception: Word Search II is Word Search run once per dictionary word.
Explanation: Running a separate board search per word repeats the same paths for every word that shares a prefix. Build a trie of the words, store each word on its end node, and run one DFS from every cell that walks the board and the trie together. Report a word when its end node is reached, then clear it so it is reported once. After exploring a node, delete it from its parent if it has no children and no word left, so later searches never enter finished branches.
Incorrect:
// One full board search per word: shared prefixes are explored again and again.
return words.filter(w => existsOnBoard(board, w));
Correct:
function findWords(board, words) {
  const root = { children: new Map(), word: null };
  for (const w of words) {
    let node = root;
    for (const ch of w) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, { children: new Map(), word: null });
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    node.word = w;
  }
  const m = board.length, n = board[0].length, found = [];
  const dfs = (r, c, parent) => {
    const ch = board[r][c];
    const node = parent.children.get(ch);
    if (!node) return;
    if (node.word !== null) { found.push(node.word); node.word = null; }
    board[r][c] = '#';
    for (const [dr, dc] of [[1, 0], [-1, 0], [0, 1], [0, -1]]) {
      const nr = r + dr, nc = c + dc;
      if (nr >= 0 && nr < m && nc >= 0 && nc < n && board[nr][nc] !== '#') dfs(nr, nc, node);
    }
    board[r][c] = ch;
    if (node.children.size === 0 && node.word === null) parent.children.delete(ch);
  };
  for (let r = 0; r < m; r++) for (let c = 0; c < n; c++) dfs(r, c, root);
  return found;
}
Complexity: O(m · n · 4 · 3^(L-1)) worst case; pruning removes most of it in practice

