JavaScript · union_find · mutation · L0
Concept: A group is identified by its root, so union and connectivity both work on roots
Misconception: parent[a] = b merges a's and b's groups, and parent[a] === parent[b] means they are connected.
Explanation: Union-find stores each group as a tree in a parent array, and only the root, the node that is its own parent, identifies the group. Setting parent[a] = b moves only a and leaves the rest of a's group behind. Two members of one group can have different direct parents, so comparing parent[a] with parent[b] gives false negatives. Always call find on both sides, link one root under the other, and compare roots.
Incorrect:
function union(a, b) { parent[a] = b; }
function connected(a, b) { return parent[a] === parent[b]; }
Correct:
const parent = Array.from({ length: 4 }, (_, i) => i);
function find(x) {
  while (parent[x] !== x) x = parent[x];
  return x;
}
function union(a, b) {
  const ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
  if (ra === rb) return false;
  parent[ra] = rb;
  return true;
}
const connected = (a, b) => find(a) === find(b);
Complexity: O(depth) per find without optimisations

