JavaScript · union_find · complexity · L1
Concept: Path compression plus union by size make union-find nearly O(1) per operation
Misconception: Union-find is fast with or without its optimisations.
Explanation: Without optimisations, unions can build a chain, and find walks the whole chain: O(n) per call. Union by size attaches the smaller tree under the larger, which keeps trees shallow. Path compression makes every node visited by find point to the root (or, with halving, to its grandparent), so later finds take one step. Together they give an amortised cost bounded by the inverse Ackermann function, which is at most 4 for any realistic input. Write find iteratively, because a recursive find on a long chain throws RangeError before it gets the chance to compress anything.
Incorrect:
const find = x => (parent[x] === x ? x : find(parent[x])); // no compression, recursive
Correct:
const n = 8;
const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);
const size = new Array(n).fill(1);
function find(x) {
  let root = x;
  while (parent[root] !== root) root = parent[root];
  while (parent[x] !== root) { const next = parent[x]; parent[x] = root; x = next; }
  return root;
}
function union(a, b) {
  let ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
  if (ra === rb) return false;
  if (size[ra] < size[rb]) [ra, rb] = [rb, ra];
  parent[rb] = ra;
  size[ra] += size[rb];
  return true;
}
Complexity: Nearly O(1) amortised per operation (inverse Ackermann)

