JavaScript · array_patterns · patterns · L0
Concept: Two pointers from both ends find a pair in a sorted array in O(n)
Misconception: Finding two numbers that add up to a target needs a nested loop, or a sort followed by a search for each element.
Explanation: When the array is sorted, put one pointer at each end and look at the sum. If it is too small, only moving the left pointer right can increase it; if it is too large, only moving the right pointer left can decrease it. Each step discards one element for good, so the scan is O(n) time and O(1) space. The trick depends on sorted order; on unsorted input sort a copy first (and keep original indexes if the answer needs them) or use a Map.
Incorrect:
function pairSum(a, t) {
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    for (let j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++)
      if (a[i] + a[j] === t) return [i, j];
  return null;
}
Correct:
function pairSum(a, t) {
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const s = a[lo] + a[hi];
    if (s === t) return [lo, hi];
    if (s < t) lo++; else hi--;
  }
  return null;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space on sorted input

