JavaScript · array_patterns · edge-cases · L1
Concept: Skip equal neighbours after sorting so two-pointer searches return each combination once
Misconception: A set of stringified triples is the natural way to remove duplicate results from 3Sum.
Explanation: Sort with a numeric comparator first (the default sort compares strings, so [10, 9, 1] would not be ordered). Fix the first element with an outer loop, run opposite pointers on the rest, and after every hit or move step past values equal to the one just used. Skip a repeated outer value too. Duplicates are never produced, so no set is needed, and the work stays O(n squared).
Incorrect:
const seen = new Set(), out = [];
// ... find triples, then:
if (!seen.has(String(t))) { seen.add(String(t)); out.push(t); }
Correct:
function threeSum(nums) {
  const a = [...nums].sort((x, y) => x - y), out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length - 2; i++) {
    if (i > 0 && a[i] === a[i - 1]) continue;
    let lo = i + 1, hi = a.length - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const s = a[i] + a[lo] + a[hi];
      if (s < 0) lo++;
      else if (s > 0) hi--;
      else {
        out.push([a[i], a[lo], a[hi]]);
        lo++; hi--;
        while (lo < hi && a[lo] === a[lo - 1]) lo++;
        while (lo < hi && a[hi] === a[hi + 1]) hi--;
      }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n^2) time, O(1) extra besides the sorted copy

