Concept: A variable window grows on the right and shrinks from the left until its rule holds again
Misconception: When a window breaks the rule, restart the scan from the next index.
Explanation: Expand the right edge one element at a time and record it. While the window is invalid, advance the left edge and undo its effect. Both edges only move forward, so the loop is O(n) even though it contains a while. Store the last-seen index in a Map to jump the left edge directly, but never move it backwards: use Math.max(left, last + 1). This works when removing an element can only make an invalid window better, as with non-negative numbers or distinct-character rules; with negative numbers use prefix sums instead.
Incorrect:
function longestUnique(s) {
  let best = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    const seen = new Set();
    for (let j = i; j < s.length && !seen.has(s[j]); j++) seen.add(s[j]);
    best = Math.max(best, seen.size);
  }
  return best;
}
Correct:
function longestUnique(s) {
  const last = new Map();
  let left = 0, best = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
    const ch = s[right];
    if (last.has(ch)) left = Math.max(left, last.get(ch) + 1);
    last.set(ch, right);
    best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
  }
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(alphabet) space

