Concept: Count subarrays with a target sum by storing how often each prefix sum has occurred
Misconception: A sliding window counts subarrays that sum to k, even when the array has negative numbers.
Explanation: A subarray ending here sums to k when an earlier prefix equals current - k. Keep a Map from prefix sum to count, seeded with {0: 1} so subarrays that start at index 0 are counted, and add the count before inserting the current prefix. Windows fail with negatives because adding an element can lower the sum, so shrinking is no longer monotonic. Keys are numbers, so 0 and -0 are the same Map key (SameValueZero) and no special handling is needed.
Incorrect:
function countK(a, k) {
  let left = 0, sum = 0, count = 0;
  for (let right = 0; right < a.length; right++) {
    sum += a[right];
    while (sum > k) sum -= a[left++];
    if (sum === k) count++;
  }
  return count;
}
Correct:
function countK(a, k) {
  const seen = new Map([[0, 1]]);
  let sum = 0, count = 0;
  for (const x of a) {
    sum += x;
    count += seen.get(sum - k) ?? 0;
    seen.set(sum, (seen.get(sum) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  return count;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

