Concept: A lower-bound search returns the first index that satisfies a monotone condition
Misconception: Binary search should return as soon as it finds the target, which is enough for first and last occurrences too.
Explanation: Searching for a boundary is more general than searching for a value. Use a half-open range [lo, hi) with hi = length, and while lo < hi test a condition that flips once from false to true. If mid fails the condition, lo = mid + 1; otherwise hi = mid. When the loop ends, lo is the first index that satisfies it, or length if none does. Lower bound is first index with a[i] >= x; upper bound is first index with a[i] > x; the number of occurrences is the difference.
Incorrect:
function firstIndex(a, x) {
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (a[mid] === x) return mid; // any occurrence, not the first
    if (a[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
Correct:
function lowerBound(a, x) {
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (a[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;
  }
  return lo;
}
