Concept: Binary search can run over answer values when feasibility is monotone
Misconception: Binary search only applies to sorted arrays.
Explanation: If you can test "is answer x enough?" in O(n), and larger x never makes a feasible answer infeasible, the answers are sorted by feasibility even though there is no array. Search the smallest feasible x between a lower and an upper bound: the largest element for a minimum speed, the sum for a minimum capacity. Use Math.ceil for divisions that must round up. Total cost is O(n log range), and each probe is a simple check function.
Incorrect:
function minSpeed(piles, h) {
  for (let k = 1; ; k++) {            // linear scan over answers
    let hours = 0;
    for (const p of piles) hours += Math.ceil(p / k);
    if (hours <= h) return k;
  }
}
Correct:
function minSpeed(piles, h) {
  let lo = 1, hi = Math.max(...piles);
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    let hours = 0;
    for (const p of piles) hours += Math.ceil(p / mid);
    if (hours <= h) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
  }
  return lo;
}
Complexity: O(n log max) time, O(1) space

