Concept: Two pointers from both ends find a pair in a sorted array in O(n)
Misconception: Finding two numbers that add up to a target needs a nested loop.
Explanation: On a sorted array, put one index at each end and look at the sum. Too small means only moving the left index right can help; too large means only moving the right index left can help. Each step discards one element, so the scan is O(n) time and O(1) space. Declare the sum as long when the values can be large, because int addition wraps silently and a wrapped negative sum sends a pointer the wrong way. On unsorted input, sort a copy with Arrays.sort or use a HashMap.
Incorrect:
static int[] pairSum(int[] a, int t) {
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++)
      if (a[i] + a[j] == t) return new int[]{i, j};
  return null;
}
Correct:
static int[] pairSum(int[] a, long t) {
  int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {
    long s = (long) a[lo] + a[hi];
    if (s == t) return new int[]{lo, hi};
    if (s < t) lo++; else hi--;
  }
  return null;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space on sorted input

