Concept: Skip equal neighbours after sorting so two-pointer searches return each combination once
Misconception: A HashSet of lists is the natural way to remove duplicate results from 3Sum.
Explanation: Sort a copy, fix the first element with an outer loop, run opposite pointers on the rest, and after each hit step past values equal to the ones just used. Skip a repeated outer value as well. No duplicate triple is ever built, so no set is needed and the work stays O(n squared). Compute sums as long when inputs can approach the int limits, since a wrapped sum looks like a valid small one. Arrays.sort on int[] is a dual-pivot quicksort and needs no comparator.
Incorrect:
Set<List<Integer>> seen = new HashSet<>();
// ... find triples, then:
seen.add(List.of(a[i], a[lo], a[hi])); // builds and hashes every triple, even repeats
Correct:
static List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {
  int[] a = nums.clone();
  Arrays.sort(a);
  List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 2; i++) {
    if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;
    int lo = i + 1, hi = a.length - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
      long s = (long) a[i] + a[lo] + a[hi];
      if (s < 0) lo++;
      else if (s > 0) hi--;
      else {
        out.add(List.of(a[i], a[lo], a[hi]));
        lo++; hi--;
        while (lo < hi && a[lo] == a[lo - 1]) lo++;
        while (lo < hi && a[hi] == a[hi + 1]) hi--;
      }
    }
  }
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n^2) time, O(1) extra besides the sorted copy

