Concept: Count subarrays with a target sum by storing how often each prefix sum has occurred
Misconception: A sliding window counts subarrays that sum to k, even with negative numbers.
Explanation: A subarray ending here sums to k when an earlier prefix equals current - k. Keep a HashMap<Long, Integer> from prefix to count, seeded with 0 -> 1, and read the count before inserting the current prefix. Use long keys so a large prefix is not silently wrapped, and getOrDefault to avoid null unboxing. Windows fail with negatives because adding an element can lower the sum, so shrinking is not monotonic.
Incorrect:
static int countK(int[] a, int k) {
  int left = 0, sum = 0, count = 0;
  for (int right = 0; right < a.length; right++) {
    sum += a[right];
    while (sum > k) sum -= a[left++];
    if (sum == k) count++;
  }
  return count;
}
Correct:
static int countK(int[] a, long k) {
  Map<Long, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
  seen.put(0L, 1);
  long sum = 0;
  int count = 0;
  for (int x : a) {
    sum += x;
    count += seen.getOrDefault(sum - k, 0);
    seen.merge(sum, 1, Integer::sum);
  }
  return count;
}
