Concept: The midpoint (lo + hi) / 2 can overflow int, so write lo + (hi - lo) / 2
Misconception: (lo + hi) / 2 is always safe because both values are valid array indexes.
Explanation: Java int addition wraps at 2^31 - 1. With lo and hi both above a billion, lo + hi becomes negative and the index is invalid. This bug lived in the JDK's own binary search for years. Use lo + (hi - lo) / 2 or (lo + hi) >>> 1; the unsigned shift treats the wrapped sum as a 32-bit positive number. The same applies when binary searching an int answer range such as 1 to 2 billion, even though array sizes are smaller. Pick one loop invariant and keep it in every branch.
Incorrect:
int mid = (lo + hi) / 2; // negative when lo + hi passes Integer.MAX_VALUE
Correct:
static int find(int[] a, int x) {
  int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == x) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(log n) time, O(1) space

