Concept: Binary search can run over answer values when feasibility is monotone
Misconception: Binary search only applies to sorted arrays, and Math.ceil(p / k) rounds up integer division.
Explanation: If "is answer x enough?" is an O(n) check and larger x never turns a feasible answer infeasible, binary search the smallest feasible x between a lower and an upper bound. In Java, p / k on ints truncates before Math.ceil can see it, so use (p + k - 1) / k, or Math.ceilDiv from Java 18 on (this curriculum targets 17, so use the manual form). Accumulate the check's total in a long. Cost is O(n log range).
Incorrect:
hours += (int) Math.ceil(p / mid); // p / mid is already truncated
Correct:
static int minSpeed(int[] piles, int h) {
  int lo = 1, hi = 0;
  for (int p : piles) hi = Math.max(hi, p);
  while (lo < hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    long hours = 0;
    for (int p : piles) hours += (p + mid - 1) / mid;
    if (hours <= h) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
  }
  return lo;
}
Complexity: O(n log max) time, O(1) space

