Concept: Counting and bucket sort beat O(n log n) when keys are small integers or frequencies
Misconception: Top-k frequent elements needs a sort, or a heap, at O(n log n).
Explanation: Frequencies are bounded by n, so bucket by count: build a Map of counts, then an array of n + 1 lists where index c holds the values that occurred c times, and read from the high end. Time is O(n). The same idea sorts small non-negative integers directly: count each value, then emit them in order. It does not apply when the key range is huge compared with n. Use new Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => []), because fill([]) would share one list.
Incorrect:
