Concept: Merge sort counts inversions during the merge step
Misconception: Counting pairs that are out of order needs a nested loop at O(n squared).
Explanation: Split the array, sort both halves recursively, then merge. When the next element comes from the right half while i elements of the left half remain, each of those remaining left elements is greater than it, so add the number of remaining left elements to the count. Total time is O(n log n) and merge sort is stable. Use strict comparison on ties: taking the left element when equal (<=) keeps stability and avoids counting equal pairs. Allocate the merge buffer once for large inputs; recursion depth is only log n.
Incorrect:
let inv = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
  for (let j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++) if (a[i] > a[j]) inv++;
Correct:
function countInversions(a) {
  let inv = 0;
  const sort = (arr) => {
    if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;
    const mid = arr.length >> 1;
    const L = sort(arr.slice(0, mid)), R = sort(arr.slice(mid));
    const out = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < L.length && j < R.length) {
      if (L[i] <= R[j]) out.push(L[i++]);
      else { inv += L.length - i; out.push(R[j++]); }
    }
    while (i < L.length) out.push(L[i++]);
    while (j < R.length) out.push(R[j++]);
    return out;
  };
  sort(a);
  return inv;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

