Concept: Quickselect finds the k-th element in average O(n) by partitioning one side only
Misconception: Finding the k-th largest requires sorting, or a heap of size k.
Explanation: Partition around a pivot; the pivot lands at its final index p. If p equals the target index, done; otherwise recurse (loop) into only the side that contains it. Average cost is O(n); a bad pivot on sorted input makes it O(n squared), so pick the pivot at random. Quickselect rearranges the array, so copy it if the caller needs the original. A heap of size k is O(n log k) and the right choice for streaming data or when the array must not be mutated.
Incorrect:
const kthLargest = (a, k) => [...a].sort((x, y) => y - x)[k - 1]; // O(n log n)
Correct:
function kthLargest(nums, k) {
  const a = [...nums], target = a.length - k;
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const r = lo + Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1));
    [a[r], a[hi]] = [a[hi], a[r]];
    const pivot = a[hi];
    let p = lo;
    for (let i = lo; i < hi; i++) if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[i], a[p]] = [a[p], a[i]]; p++; }
    [a[p], a[hi]] = [a[hi], a[p]];
    if (p === target) return a[p];
    if (p < target) lo = p + 1; else hi = p - 1;
  }
  return a[lo];
}
Complexity: O(n) average, O(n^2) worst, O(n) space for the copy

