Concept: In a rotated sorted array one half is always sorted, so decide which half holds the target
Misconception: Binary search cannot work once a sorted array has been rotated.
Explanation: At any mid, at least one of [lo, mid] and [mid, hi] is sorted. Compare a[lo] with a[mid] to find the sorted half, check whether the target lies inside that half's range, and discard the other half. This needs distinct values: with duplicates a[lo] === a[mid] tells you nothing and the worst case degrades to O(n). Use <= when comparing a[lo] with a[mid], because lo and mid can be the same index.
Incorrect:
const find = (a, x) => a.indexOf(x); // O(n)
Correct:
function searchRotated(a, x) {
  let lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
    if (a[mid] === x) return mid;
    if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {
      if (a[lo] <= x && x < a[mid]) hi = mid - 1; else lo = mid + 1;
    } else if (a[mid] < x && x <= a[hi]) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(log n) time for distinct values, O(1) space

