Concept: Start at a corner of a row- and column-sorted matrix to discard a row or column each step
Misconception: Searching a sorted matrix needs a binary search per row.
Explanation: When rows and columns both ascend, the top-right cell is the largest in its row and the smallest in its column. If it is greater than the target, drop its column; if smaller, drop its row. Each step removes a whole line, so the walk takes at most rows + cols steps, O(m + n). If each row also starts above the previous row's last element, the matrix is one sorted sequence and a single binary search over index / cols and index % cols gives O(log(m*n)). Check for an empty matrix and empty rows before reading matrix[0].length.
