Java · sorting · patterns · L1
Concept: Counting and bucket sort beat O(n log n) when keys are small integers or frequencies
Misconception: Top-k frequent elements needs a sort or a priority queue, and List<Integer>[] is a fine bucket array.
Explanation: Frequencies are bounded by n, so bucket by count: build a HashMap of counts, then a List<List<Integer>> with n + 1 lists where index c holds the values seen c times, and read from the high end. Time is O(n). Creating a generic array such as new List<Integer>[n + 1] does not compile; use a list of lists. For small non-negative int keys, an int[] of counts is both the fastest map and the sorted order. It does not apply when the key range is huge compared with n.
Incorrect:
Map<Integer, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
for (int x : nums) counts.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);
// then sort the entries by value: O(n log n)
Correct:
static List<Integer> topK(int[] nums, int k) {
  Map<Integer, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
  for (int x : nums) counts.merge(x, 1, Integer::sum);
  List<List<Integer>> buckets = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int i = 0; i <= nums.length; i++) buckets.add(new ArrayList<>());
  for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> e : counts.entrySet()) buckets.get(e.getValue()).add(e.getKey());
  List<Integer> out = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int c = buckets.size() - 1; c > 0 && out.size() < k; c--) out.addAll(buckets.get(c));
  return out.subList(0, Math.min(k, out.size()));
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

