Java · sorting · patterns · L1
Concept: Three-way partition sorts values 0, 1 and 2 in one pass with three indexes
Misconception: After swapping with the high index, advance the middle index like any other swap.
Explanation: Keep low (next slot for 0), mid (current) and high (next slot for 2). If a[mid] is 0, swap with low and advance both; if 1, advance mid; if 2, swap with high and shrink high, but do not advance mid, because the value that arrived from high has not been examined. One pass, O(n) time, O(1) space. Java has no destructuring swap, so use a small helper or a temporary variable. The same partition handles less-than, equal and greater-than a pivot.
Incorrect:
if (a[mid] == 2) { swap(a, mid, high); high--; mid++; } // skips the swapped-in value
Correct:
static void swap(int[] a, int i, int j) { int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t; }
static int[] sortColors(int[] a) {
  int low = 0, mid = 0, high = a.length - 1;
  while (mid <= high) {
    if (a[mid] == 0) swap(a, low++, mid++);
    else if (a[mid] == 1) mid++;
    else swap(a, mid, high--);
  }
  return a;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

