Java · sorting · patterns · L2
Concept: Merge sort counts inversions during the merge step, and the count needs a long
Misconception: The number of inversions fits in an int because the array length does.
Explanation: An array of n elements has up to n(n-1)/2 inversions. For n = 70,000 in reverse order that is 2,449,965,000, above Integer.MAX_VALUE, so an int counter wraps silently. Count in a long. When the next element comes from the right half while i elements of the left half remain, add (mid - i) to the count. Take the left element on ties (<=) to keep the sort stable and to ignore equal pairs. Use one auxiliary array allocated once, not a new array per merge.
Incorrect:
int inv = 0;                       // wraps for large reversed input
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
  for (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++) if (a[i] > a[j]) inv++;
Correct:
static long countInversions(int[] a) {
  return sort(a.clone(), new int[a.length], 0, a.length);
}
private static long sort(int[] a, int[] buf, int lo, int hi) {
  if (hi - lo <= 1) return 0;
  int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
  long inv = sort(a, buf, lo, mid) + sort(a, buf, mid, hi);
  int i = lo, j = mid, k = lo;
  while (i < mid && j < hi) {
    if (a[i] <= a[j]) buf[k++] = a[i++];
    else { inv += mid - i; buf[k++] = a[j++]; }
  }
  while (i < mid) buf[k++] = a[i++];
  while (j < hi) buf[k++] = a[j++];
  System.arraycopy(buf, lo, a, lo, hi - lo);
  return inv;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

