Java · sorting · patterns · L2
Concept: Quickselect finds the k-th element in average O(n) by partitioning one side only
Misconception: Finding the k-th largest requires sorting, or a heap of size k.
Explanation: Partition around a pivot; the pivot lands at its final index p. If p equals the target index, done; otherwise loop into only the side that contains it. Average cost is O(n); with a fixed pivot, sorted input degrades to O(n squared), so pick the pivot at random (ThreadLocalRandom). Quickselect reorders the array, so clone it if the caller still needs the original. A PriorityQueue of size k is O(n log k) and suits streams or arrays that must not be touched.
Incorrect:
static int kthLargest(int[] a, int k) {
  int[] c = a.clone();
  Arrays.sort(c);                 // O(n log n)
  return c[c.length - k];
}
Correct:
static int kthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
  int[] a = nums.clone();
  int target = a.length - k, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom rnd = java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom.current();
  while (lo < hi) {
    int r = lo + rnd.nextInt(hi - lo + 1);
    int t = a[r]; a[r] = a[hi]; a[hi] = t;
    int pivot = a[hi], p = lo;
    for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++) if (a[i] < pivot) { t = a[i]; a[i] = a[p]; a[p] = t; p++; }
    t = a[p]; a[p] = a[hi]; a[hi] = t;
    if (p == target) return a[p];
    if (p < target) lo = p + 1; else hi = p - 1;
  }
  return a[lo];
}
Complexity: O(n) average, O(n^2) worst, O(n) space for the clone

