Java · sorting · patterns · L2
Concept: In a rotated sorted array one half is always sorted, so decide which half holds the target
Misconception: Binary search cannot work once a sorted array has been rotated, and Arrays.binarySearch still applies.
Explanation: Arrays.binarySearch assumes the whole array is sorted and returns garbage on a rotated one. At any mid, at least one of [lo, mid] and [mid, hi] is sorted. Compare a[lo] with a[mid] to find the sorted half, check whether the target lies in that half's range, and discard the other half. This needs distinct values: with duplicates, a[lo] == a[mid] tells you nothing and the worst case becomes O(n). Use <= for a[lo] versus a[mid], because lo and mid can be the same index.
Incorrect:
int i = Arrays.binarySearch(new int[]{4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2}, 0); // undefined result: input is not sorted
Correct:
static int searchRotated(int[] a, int x) {
  int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == x) return mid;
    if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {
      if (a[lo] <= x && x < a[mid]) hi = mid - 1; else lo = mid + 1;
    } else if (a[mid] < x && x <= a[hi]) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(log n) time for distinct values, O(1) space

