Java · sorting · patterns · L1
Concept: Start at a corner of a row- and column-sorted matrix to discard a row or column each step
Misconception: Searching a sorted matrix needs a binary search per row.
Explanation: When rows and columns both ascend, the top-right cell is the largest in its row and the smallest in its column. If it is greater than the target, drop its column; if smaller, drop its row. Each step removes a whole line, so the walk takes at most rows + cols steps, O(m + n). If each row also starts above the previous row's last element, one binary search over index / cols and index % cols gives O(log(m*n)). Check m.length == 0 and m[0].length == 0 before reading m[0], or the code throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.
Incorrect:
for (int[] row : m) if (Arrays.binarySearch(row, t) >= 0) return true; // O(m log n), ignores column order
Correct:
static boolean searchMatrix(int[][] m, int t) {
  if (m.length == 0 || m[0].length == 0) return false;
  int r = 0, c = m[0].length - 1;
  while (r < m.length && c >= 0) {
    if (m[r][c] == t) return true;
    if (m[r][c] > t) c--; else r++;
  }
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(m + n) time, O(1) space

