Concept: Every recursion needs a base case, and JavaScript's call stack allows only about ten thousand frames
Misconception: Recursion depth is only limited by memory, so a recursive function over a list of 100,000 items is fine.
Explanation: Each call adds a stack frame, and V8 throws RangeError (Maximum call stack size exceeded) at roughly ten thousand frames, depending on frame size. Tail calls are not optimised in practice. A recursion that is correct for 1,000 items can crash at 100,000. Write the base case first, make sure each call moves toward it, and when depth can grow with the input, switch to a loop or an explicit stack. Recursion over a balanced tree is safe because depth is log n; over a list or a degenerate tree it is not.
Incorrect:
const depth = (n) => (n === 0 ? 0 : 1 + depth(n - 1));
depth(100000); // RangeError
Correct:
function depth(n) {
  let count = 0;
  while (n > 0) { count++; n--; }
  return count;
}
Complexity: O(n) time; recursion uses O(n) stack, the loop uses O(1)

