JavaScript · recursion_dp · complexity · L1
Concept: Memoise overlapping subproblems in a Map, and use BigInt once values pass 2^53
Misconception: Fibonacci with a cache is exact for any n, because JavaScript numbers are big enough.
Explanation: Plain recursion recomputes the same subproblems, which makes fib exponential. Cache each result by its arguments and the cost drops to O(n). A Map handles any key you can build; for several arguments join them into a string such as `${i},${j}`. Numbers are doubles, exact only up to 2^53 - 1: fib(80) is 23416728348467685 but the Number version prints ...684. Use BigInt for exact large results, and keep the values in the Map as BigInt too, since BigInt and Number cannot be mixed in arithmetic.
Incorrect:
function fib(n) {
  let a = 0, b = 1;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) [a, b] = [b, a + b];
  return a; // fib(80) is 23416728348467684: not exact
}
Correct:
function fib(n, memo = new Map()) {
  if (n < 2) return BigInt(n);
  if (memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n);
  const v = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);
  memo.set(n, v);
  return v;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

