JavaScript · recursion_dp · mutation · L1
Concept: Backtracking pushes a choice, recurses, pops it, and copies the path when recording
Misconception: out.push(path) records the current path.
Explanation: The path array is one object that every call mutates. Pushing path itself stores a reference, so after the search finishes every recorded entry is the same, empty, array. Record a copy ([...path]) at the moment you reach a result. The pattern is choose, explore, undo: for each element either skip it or push it, recurse, then pop. Subsets of n items number 2^n, so the cost is O(n * 2^n) because of the copies.
Incorrect:
function subsets(nums) {
  const out = [], path = [];
  (function go(i) {
    if (i === nums.length) { out.push(path); return; } // same array every time
    go(i + 1);
    path.push(nums[i]); go(i + 1); path.pop();
  })(0);
  return out; // [[], [], [], []]
}
Correct:
function subsets(nums) {
  const out = [], path = [];
  (function go(i) {
    if (i === nums.length) { out.push([...path]); return; }
    go(i + 1);
    path.push(nums[i]); go(i + 1); path.pop();
  })(0);
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n * 2^n) time, O(n) recursion depth

