JavaScript · recursion_dp · edge-cases · L2
Concept: Skip a duplicate value unless its equal left neighbour is already in use
Misconception: Permutations of an array with repeated values need a Set of stringified results to remove duplicates.
Explanation: Sort the input and keep a used flag per position. At each depth, skip index i when it is used, and skip it when a[i] equals a[i - 1] and a[i - 1] is not currently used: that means the equal twin was already tried at this position. This prunes duplicate branches instead of generating and discarding them. Without sorting, equal values are not adjacent and the rule fails. The count is n! divided by the product of the factorials of the repeat counts.
Incorrect:
const seen = new Set();
// ... build every permutation, then:
seen.add(JSON.stringify(path)); // generates all n! permutations first
Correct:
function permuteUnique(nums) {
  const a = [...nums].sort((x, y) => x - y);
  const used = new Array(a.length).fill(false), out = [], path = [];
  (function go() {
    if (path.length === a.length) { out.push([...path]); return; }
    for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
      if (used[i]) continue;
      if (i > 0 && a[i] === a[i - 1] && !used[i - 1]) continue;
      used[i] = true; path.push(a[i]);
      go();
      path.pop(); used[i] = false;
    }
  })();
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n * n!) worst case

