Concept: A start index prevents reordered duplicates, and sorting lets you stop a loop early
Misconception: Combination sum can loop over all candidates at every step.
Explanation: If every step loops from index 0, [2, 3] and [3, 2] are both produced. Pass a start index and loop from it, so a combination is built in index order. To allow reuse of the same number, recurse with i, not i + 1. Sort the candidates and break (not continue) as soon as one exceeds the remaining target, since every later candidate is larger. Copy the path when the remainder is 0.
Incorrect:
function go(rem) {
  if (rem === 0) { out.push([...path]); return; }
  for (const c of candidates) { // starts at 0 each time: [2,3,2] and [2,2,3] both appear
    if (c > rem) continue;
    path.push(c); go(rem - c); path.pop();
  }
}
Correct:
function combinationSum(candidates, target) {
  const a = [...candidates].sort((x, y) => x - y), out = [], path = [];
  (function go(start, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) { out.push([...path]); return; }
    for (let i = start; i < a.length; i++) {
      if (a[i] > rem) break;
      path.push(a[i]); go(i, rem - a[i]); path.pop();
    }
  })(0, target);
  return out;
}
Complexity: Exponential in target / smallest candidate; pruning cuts most branches

