Concept: Define the state first, then keep only the last values the recurrence reads
Misconception: Pick the larger of the even-index sum and odd-index sum to solve house robber.
Explanation: A DP needs a state meaning, a recurrence and a base. For house robber, best(i) is the most you can take from the first i houses, and best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + a[i]). Because the recurrence reads only two earlier values, two variables replace the array and space drops to O(1). Choosing all even or all odd positions fails on [2, 1, 1, 2], where the best is 4 (first and last) and each parity gives 3. Write the recurrence before the code.
Incorrect:
function rob(a) {
  let even = 0, odd = 0;
  a.forEach((x, i) => (i % 2 ? (odd += x) : (even += x)));
  return Math.max(even, odd); // 3 for [2, 1, 1, 2], answer is 4
}
Correct:
function rob(a) {
  let prev = 0, cur = 0;
  for (const x of a) [prev, cur] = [cur, Math.max(cur, prev + x)];
  return cur;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

