Concept: Build a two-dimensional DP table with independent rows and a zero border
Misconception: Array(m + 1).fill(Array(n + 1).fill(0)) creates a table of zeros I can fill.
Explanation: fill with an array puts the same array object in every row, so writing dp[1][1] changes dp[i][1] for every i. Create each row separately with Array.from. For longest common subsequence, dp[i][j] is the length for the first i characters of a and the first j of b; the extra row and column of zeros are the empty-prefix base case, so there are no index -1 reads. If characters match take dp[i-1][j-1] + 1, otherwise the larger of dp[i-1][j] and dp[i][j-1]. A row at a time is enough if only the previous row is read.
Incorrect:
const dp = new Array(a.length + 1).fill(new Array(b.length + 1).fill(0)); // one shared row
Correct:
function lcs(a, b) {
  const dp = Array.from({ length: a.length + 1 }, () => new Array(b.length + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= a.length; i++)
    for (let j = 1; j <= b.length; j++)
      dp[i][j] = a[i - 1] === b[j - 1] ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1 : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
  return dp[a.length][b.length];
}
Complexity: O(m * n) time and space; O(min(m, n)) space with rolling rows

