JavaScript · recursion_dp · complexity · L3
Concept: Longest increasing subsequence drops from O(n squared) to O(n log n) with a tails array and lower bound
Misconception: The tails array is the longest increasing subsequence.
Explanation: tails[k] is the smallest possible last value of an increasing subsequence of length k + 1. For each x, find the first index whose tail is >= x (lower bound) and overwrite it, or append when x is larger than every tail. The length of tails is the answer, but the array itself is generally not a valid subsequence. Use lower bound for a strictly increasing result; use upper bound if equal values may repeat in a non-decreasing subsequence.
Incorrect:
function lis(a) {
  const dp = new Array(a.length).fill(1);
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) if (a[j] < a[i]) dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
  return Math.max(0, ...dp); // O(n^2)
}
Correct:
function lis(a) {
  const tails = [];
  for (const x of a) {
    let lo = 0, hi = tails.length;
    while (lo < hi) {
      const mid = lo + Math.floor((hi - lo) / 2);
      if (tails[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;
    }
    tails[lo] = x;
  }
  return tails.length;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

