Java · recursion_dp · edge-cases · L0
Concept: Deep recursion throws StackOverflowError, which catch (Exception e) does not catch
Misconception: A try/catch (Exception e) around a deep recursion will handle the failure.
Explanation: The default thread stack allows roughly ten to twenty thousand frames for a simple method. Past that the JVM throws StackOverflowError, which is an Error and not an Exception, so catch (Exception e) lets it through. Do not rely on catching it; make the recursion shallow enough, or switch to a loop or an explicit Deque when depth can grow with the input. Write the base case first and make each call move toward it. Recursion over a balanced tree is safe because depth is log n; over a list or a degenerate tree it is not.
Incorrect:
static int depth(int n) { return n == 0 ? 0 : 1 + depth(n - 1); }
// depth(1_000_000) throws StackOverflowError; catch (Exception e) does not catch it
Correct:
static int depth(int n) {
  int count = 0;
  while (n > 0) { count++; n--; }
  return count;
}
Complexity: O(n) time; recursion uses O(n) stack, the loop uses O(1)

