Concept: Do not call computeIfAbsent recursively, and watch where long overflows
Misconception: map.computeIfAbsent(n, k -> fib(k - 1) + fib(k - 2)) is a neat memoised Fibonacci.
Explanation: HashMap.computeIfAbsent must not modify the map from inside its mapping function, and the recursive call does. Since Java 9 this throws ConcurrentModificationException. Check with containsKey or get, compute, then put. The result type matters as much as the cache: Fibonacci fits a long only up to fib(92) = 7540113804746346429; fib(93) wraps to a negative number with no error. Guard the input, or use BigInteger for larger n. For several arguments, use a long[][] filled with -1, or a record key.
Incorrect:
static Map<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();
static long fib(int n) {
  if (n < 2) return n;
  return memo.computeIfAbsent(n, k -> fib(k - 1) + fib(k - 2)); // ConcurrentModificationException
}
Correct:
static Map<Integer, Long> memo = new HashMap<>();
static long fib(int n) {
  if (n < 0 || n > 92) throw new IllegalArgumentException("fib(n) fits in a long only for 0..92");
  if (n < 2) return n;
  Long cached = memo.get(n);
  if (cached != null) return cached;
  long v = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
  memo.put(n, v);
  return v;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

