Concept: Backtracking adds a choice, recurses, removes it by index, and copies the path when recording
Misconception: out.add(path) records the current path, and path.remove(x) undoes the last add.
Explanation: The path list is one object that every call mutates. Adding path itself stores a reference, so after the search every recorded entry is the same, empty, list. Record a copy with new ArrayList<>(path). Undo with path.remove(path.size() - 1): on a List<Integer>, remove(int) removes by index, but remove(Object) removes the first equal value, so passing an Integer variable removes the wrong element. The pattern is choose, explore, undo. Subsets of n items number 2^n, so the cost is O(n * 2^n).
Incorrect:
static void go(int[] nums, int i, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> out) {
  if (i == nums.length) { out.add(path); return; } // same list every time
  go(nums, i + 1, path, out);
  path.add(nums[i]); go(nums, i + 1, path, out); path.remove(path.size() - 1);
}
Correct:
static List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
  List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();
  go(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), out);
  return out;
}
