Java · recursion_dp · patterns · L2
Concept: A start index prevents reordered duplicates, and sorting lets you stop a loop early
Misconception: Combination sum can loop over all candidates at every step.
Explanation: If every step loops from index 0, [2, 3] and [3, 2] are both produced. Pass a start index and loop from it, so a combination is built in index order. To allow reuse of the same number, recurse with i, not i + 1. Sort the candidates and break (not continue) as soon as one exceeds the remaining target, since every later one is larger. Copy the path when the remainder is 0. Use int remainders; targets near Integer.MAX_VALUE are not realistic here.
Incorrect:
for (int c : candidates) {            // starts at 0 each time: [2,3,2] and [2,2,3] both appear
  if (c > rem) continue;
  path.add(c); go(rem - c); path.remove(path.size() - 1);
}
Correct:
static List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
  int[] a = candidates.clone();
  Arrays.sort(a);
  List<List<Integer>> out = new ArrayList<>();
  go(a, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), out);
  return out;
}
private static void go(int[] a, int start, int rem, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> out) {
  if (rem == 0) { out.add(new ArrayList<>(path)); return; }
  for (int i = start; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (a[i] > rem) break;
    path.add(a[i]); go(a, i, rem - a[i], path, out); path.remove(path.size() - 1);
  }
}
Complexity: Exponential in target / smallest candidate; pruning cuts most branches

