Java · recursion_dp · patterns · L1
Concept: Define the state first, then keep only the last values the recurrence reads
Misconception: Pick the larger of the even-index sum and odd-index sum to solve house robber.
Explanation: A DP needs a state meaning, a recurrence and a base. For house robber, best(i) is the most you can take from the first i houses, and best(i) = max(best(i-1), best(i-2) + a[i]). The recurrence reads only two earlier values, so two variables replace the array and space drops to O(1). All even or all odd positions fails on [2, 1, 1, 2], where the best is 4 and each parity gives 3. Use long totals when values or length can push the sum past 2.1 billion.
Incorrect:
static int rob(int[] a) {
  int even = 0, odd = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) if (i % 2 == 0) even += a[i]; else odd += a[i];
  return Math.max(even, odd); // 3 for {2, 1, 1, 2}, answer is 4
}
Correct:
static long rob(int[] a) {
  long prev = 0, cur = 0;
  for (int x : a) {
    long next = Math.max(cur, prev + x);
    prev = cur;
    cur = next;
  }
  return cur;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

