Concept: Longest increasing subsequence in O(n log n) with a tails array and Arrays.binarySearch
Misconception: The tails array is the longest increasing subsequence, and a negative binarySearch result means not found.
Explanation: tails[k] is the smallest possible last value of an increasing subsequence of length k + 1. For each x, binary search the used part of tails. A found index means an equal tail exists, so overwriting it changes nothing, which is right for a strictly increasing result. A negative result is -(insertionPoint) - 1; convert it back with -(r + 1) and write x there, growing the size when the point equals it. The length is the answer, but tails itself is generally not a valid subsequence. Pass the size as the range end, because unused slots hold zeros.
Incorrect:
int i = Arrays.binarySearch(tails, x); // searches the unused zeros too, and a miss is negative
tails[i] = x;                          // ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException for negative i
Correct:
static int lis(int[] a) {
  int[] tails = new int[a.length];
  int size = 0;
  for (int x : a) {
    int i = Arrays.binarySearch(tails, 0, size, x);
    if (i < 0) i = -(i + 1);
    tails[i] = x;
    if (i == size) size++;
  }
  return size;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

