JavaScript · greedy_graphs · ordering · L1
Concept: Sort intervals by start with a numeric comparator, then extend the last merged interval with Math.max
Misconception: intervals.sort() orders pairs by their first number, and the end of a merged interval is the later interval's end.
Explanation: With no comparator, sort converts each inner array to a string, so [10, 12] sorts before [2, 3]. Compare starts numerically. After sorting, an interval overlaps the last merged one when its start is at most that interval's end; use <= if touching intervals such as [1, 2] and [2, 3] should merge. Extend with Math.max(last end, end), because a later interval can sit entirely inside the earlier one, as [2, 3] does inside [1, 10]. Push copies so the input arrays are not mutated.
Incorrect:
const a = intervals.sort();                   // string order: [[1,5],[10,12],[2,3]]
// ...and later:
last[1] = e;                                  // [[1,10],[2,3]] becomes [[1,3]]
Correct:
function merge(intervals) {
  const a = [...intervals].sort((x, y) => x[0] - y[0]);
  const out = [];
  for (const [s, e] of a) {
    const last = out[out.length - 1];
    if (last && s <= last[1]) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], e);
    else out.push([s, e]);
  }
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

