Concept: To keep the most non-overlapping intervals, sort by end time and take each interval that starts after the last end
Misconception: Sorting by start time and taking each interval that fits maximises the count.
Explanation: Finishing early leaves the most room for what follows, so sort by end and take an interval whenever its start is at or after the end of the last one taken (>= lets touching intervals coexist). Sorting by start fails: [1, 10] starts first and blocks [2, 3] and [4, 5], giving 1 instead of 2. The exchange argument: swapping any chosen interval for the one that ends earliest never makes the rest infeasible. The minimum removals to make the set non-overlapping is n minus this count.
Incorrect:
const a = [...iv].sort((x, y) => x[0] - y[0]); // by start: takes [1,10] first
Correct:
function maxNonOverlapping(iv) {
  const a = [...iv].sort((x, y) => x[1] - y[1]);
  let end = -Infinity, count = 0;
  for (const [s, e] of a) {
    if (s >= end) { count++; end = e; }
  }
  return count;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

