JavaScript · greedy_graphs · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Insert into a sorted interval list in one pass with three phases
Misconception: Insert by appending the new interval, re-sorting and merging.
Explanation: The list is already sorted and disjoint, so one pass is enough. Phase one copies intervals that end before the new one starts. Phase two absorbs every interval whose start is at most the new end, growing the new interval with min and max. Phase three copies the rest. This is O(n) rather than O(n log n) and keeps the original order of the untouched intervals. Check the empty list and insertion at either end.
Incorrect:
const out = merge([...intervals, newInterval]); // re-sorts a list that was already sorted
Correct:
function insert(intervals, [ns, ne]) {
  const out = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][1] < ns) out.push(intervals[i++]);
  let s = ns, e = ne;
  while (i < intervals.length && intervals[i][0] <= e) {
    s = Math.min(s, intervals[i][0]);
    e = Math.max(e, intervals[i][1]);
    i++;
  }
  out.push([s, e]);
  while (i < intervals.length) out.push(intervals[i++]);
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(n) space

