JavaScript · greedy_graphs · patterns · L2
Concept: Sort starts and ends separately to count the most meetings overlapping at once
Misconception: Sorting starts and ends with the default sort is fine, because they are numbers.
Explanation: The room count at any time is the number of meetings that started but not ended. Sort all starts and all ends independently. Walk the starts: if the next start is at or after the earliest unfinished end, a room frees up and is reused (advance the end pointer); otherwise a new room is needed. The final room count is the peak overlap. Use a numeric comparator: the default sort orders [1, 8, 20] as 1, 20, 8 and reports two rooms for three separated meetings. Using >= means a meeting that starts when another ends shares its room.
Incorrect:
const starts = iv.map(x => x[0]).sort();   // string order: 1, 20, 8
const ends = iv.map(x => x[1]).sort();
Correct:
function minRooms(iv) {
  const s = iv.map(x => x[0]).sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const e = iv.map(x => x[1]).sort((a, b) => a - b);
  let rooms = 0, j = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    if (s[i] >= e[j]) j++; else rooms++;
  }
  return rooms;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

