Concept: Track the farthest reachable index instead of choosing which jump to take
Misconception: Always jump to the farthest index you can see from here.
Explanation: Choosing where to land is the wrong question. Walk the array keeping far, the farthest index reachable from any index visited so far. If the current index exceeds far, you are stuck. Otherwise update far with i + a[i]. For [2, 3, 0, 1, 4], jumping to the farthest landing spot (index 2, a zero) strands you, yet index 1 reaches the end. The reach rule is correct because any index up to far is reachable. One pass, O(1) space.
Incorrect:
function canJump(a) {
  let i = 0;
  while (i < a.length - 1) {
    if (a[i] === 0) return false;
    i += a[i];            // always the longest jump: lands on index 2 (value 0) for [2,3,0,1,4]
  }
  return true;
}
Correct:
function canJump(a) {
  let far = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (i > far) return false;
    far = Math.max(far, i + a[i]);
  }
  return true;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

