JavaScript · greedy_graphs · iteration · L1
Concept: BFS gives shortest paths in an unweighted graph if you set the distance when a node is queued
Misconception: Mark a node visited when you take it off the queue, and queue.shift() is a fine dequeue.
Explanation: BFS visits nodes in order of distance, so the first time a node is reached is its shortest distance. Record the distance and mark the node when you push it, not when you pop it; otherwise the same node can be queued once per incoming edge. Initialise distances to -1, so that -1 means unvisited, and stop when the target is dequeued. Array.shift is O(n) on large arrays, so move a head index through the array instead. BFS is wrong for weighted edges; use Dijkstra there.
Incorrect:
const q = [src];
while (q.length) {
  const u = q.shift();            // O(n) per dequeue on large arrays
  if (seen.has(u)) continue;      // marked on dequeue: duplicates sit in the queue
  seen.add(u);
  for (const v of adj[u]) q.push(v);
}
Correct:
function shortestPath(n, edges, src, dst) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); adj[v].push(u); }
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  dist[src] = 0;
  const q = [src];
  for (let head = 0; head < q.length; head++) {
    const u = q[head];
    if (u === dst) return dist[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[v] === -1) { dist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.push(v); }
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

