JavaScript · greedy_graphs · edge-cases · L2
Concept: A graph is bipartite if two-colouring succeeds from every component
Misconception: Colouring from node 0 is enough to decide whether the whole graph is bipartite.
Explanation: Colour a start node 0, then give every neighbour the opposite colour; if a neighbour already has the same colour as the current node, an odd cycle exists and the answer is false. A graph can be disconnected, and an odd cycle can hide in a component that node 0 never reaches, so loop over every node and start a new BFS from each uncoloured one. Use -1 for uncoloured so that colour 0 is not mistaken for missing. Self-loops are never bipartite.
Incorrect:
function isBipartite(graph) {
  const color = new Array(graph.length).fill(-1);
  color[0] = 0;                     // only one component is checked
  const q = [0];
  for (let h = 0; h < q.length; h++) {
    const u = q[h];
    for (const v of graph[u]) {
      if (color[v] === -1) { color[v] = 1 - color[u]; q.push(v); }
      else if (color[v] === color[u]) return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
Correct:
function isBipartite(graph) {
  const color = new Array(graph.length).fill(-1);
  for (let s = 0; s < graph.length; s++) {
    if (color[s] !== -1) continue;
    color[s] = 0;
    const q = [s];
    for (let h = 0; h < q.length; h++) {
      const u = q[h];
      for (const v of graph[u]) {
        if (color[v] === -1) { color[v] = 1 - color[u]; q.push(v); }
        else if (color[v] === color[u]) return false;
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

