Java · greedy_graphs · ordering · L1
Concept: Sort int[][] with Integer.compare on the start, because Arrays.sort(int[][]) alone throws
Misconception: Arrays.sort(intervals) sorts pairs by their first number, and a - b is a safe comparator for starts.
Explanation: An int[] is not Comparable, so Arrays.sort(int[][]) with no comparator throws ClassCastException. Pass a comparator that uses Integer.compare(a[0], b[0]); the subtraction a[0] - b[0] overflows for extreme values and misorders Integer.MIN_VALUE after 1. After sorting, an interval overlaps the last merged one when its start is at most that interval's end (use <= to merge touching ones). Extend with Math.max(last end, end), because a later interval can sit entirely inside the earlier one. Convert the result with list.toArray(new int[0][]).
Incorrect:
Arrays.sort(intervals);                              // ClassCastException
Arrays.sort(intervals, (a, b) -> a[0] - b[0]);       // overflow for extreme starts
last[1] = e;                                         // [[1,10],[2,3]] becomes [[1,3]]
Correct:
static int[][] merge(int[][] intervals) {
  int[][] a = intervals.clone();
  Arrays.sort(a, (x, y) -> Integer.compare(x[0], y[0]));
  List<int[]> out = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int[] iv : a) {
    int[] last = out.isEmpty() ? null : out.get(out.size() - 1);
    if (last != null && iv[0] <= last[1]) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], iv[1]);
    else out.add(new int[]{iv[0], iv[1]});
  }
  return out.toArray(new int[0][]);
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

