Java · greedy_graphs · patterns · L2
Concept: Sort starts and ends separately to count the most meetings overlapping at once
Misconception: A start that equals an earlier end still needs a new room.
Explanation: The room count at any time is the number of meetings that started but not ended. Sort all starts and all ends independently (Arrays.sort on an int[] is numeric). Walk the starts: if the next start is at or after the earliest unfinished end, a room frees up and is reused (advance the end index); otherwise a new room is needed. The final count is the peak overlap. The comparison must be >=: with >, the touching meetings [1, 2] and [2, 3] would need two rooms. The alternative is a PriorityQueue of end times, which costs O(n log n) as well but allocates more.
Incorrect:
for (int i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
  if (s[i] > e[j]) j++; else rooms++;    // touching meetings counted as overlapping
}
Correct:
static int minRooms(int[][] iv) {
  int n = iv.length;
  int[] s = new int[n], e = new int[n];
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { s[i] = iv[i][0]; e[i] = iv[i][1]; }
  Arrays.sort(s);
  Arrays.sort(e);
  int rooms = 0, j = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (s[i] >= e[j]) j++; else rooms++;
  }
  return rooms;
}
Complexity: O(n log n) time, O(n) space

