Java · greedy_graphs · patterns · L1
Concept: Track the farthest reachable index in a long, instead of choosing which jump to take
Misconception: Always jump to the farthest index you can see, and i + a[i] fits in an int.
Explanation: Choosing where to land is the wrong question. Walk the array keeping far, the farthest index reachable from any index visited so far. If the current index exceeds far, you are stuck. Otherwise update far with i + a[i]. For {2, 3, 0, 1, 4}, jumping to the farthest landing spot (index 2, a zero) strands you, yet index 1 reaches the end. Keep far as a long: with a[i] near Integer.MAX_VALUE, i + a[i] overflows an int and goes negative, which would wrongly report a stuck position. One pass, O(1) space.
Incorrect:
static boolean canJump(int[] a) {
  int far = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (i > far) return false;
    far = Math.max(far, i + a[i]);      // i + a[i] can overflow
  }
  return true;
}
Correct:
static boolean canJump(int[] a) {
  long far = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (i > far) return false;
    far = Math.max(far, (long) i + a[i]);
  }
  return true;
}
Complexity: O(n) time, O(1) space

