Java · greedy_graphs · iteration · L1
Concept: Initialise BFS distances to -1, because 0 is the source's own distance
Misconception: A new int[n] distance array can use 0 to mean unvisited.
Explanation: BFS visits nodes in order of distance, so the first time a node is reached is its shortest distance. Record the distance when you enqueue the node. A fresh int[n] holds zeros, and 0 is also the source's distance, so the source looks unvisited and gets queued again with distance 2. Fill with -1 and test for -1. Use ArrayDeque as the queue: it rejects null elements and is faster than LinkedList. BFS is wrong for weighted edges; use Dijkstra there.
Incorrect:
int[] dist = new int[n];            // zeros: the source looks unvisited
q.add(src);
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
  int u = q.poll();
  for (int v : adj.get(u)) if (dist[v] == 0) { dist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.add(v); }
}
Correct:
static int shortestPath(int n, int[][] edges, int src, int dst) {
  List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
  for (int[] e : edges) { adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]); adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]); }
  int[] dist = new int[n];
  Arrays.fill(dist, -1);
  dist[src] = 0;
  Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
  q.add(src);
  while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    int u = q.poll();
    if (u == dst) return dist[u];
    for (int v : adj.get(u)) {
      if (dist[v] == -1) { dist[v] = dist[u] + 1; q.add(v); }
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

