Java · greedy_graphs · edge-cases · L2
Concept: A graph is bipartite if two-colouring succeeds from every component
Misconception: Colouring from node 0 is enough to decide whether the whole graph is bipartite.
Explanation: Colour a start node 0, then give every neighbour the opposite colour; if a neighbour already has the same colour as the current node, an odd cycle exists and the answer is false. A graph can be disconnected, and an odd cycle can hide in a component that node 0 never reaches, so loop over every node and start a new BFS from each uncoloured one. Use -1 for uncoloured so colour 0 is not mistaken for missing; a new int[n] holds zeros. Self-loops are never bipartite.
Incorrect:
int[] color = new int[graph.length]; // zeros: every node already looks coloured 0
// ... BFS from node 0 only
Correct:
static boolean isBipartite(int[][] graph) {
  int[] color = new int[graph.length];
  Arrays.fill(color, -1);
  for (int s = 0; s < graph.length; s++) {
    if (color[s] != -1) continue;
    color[s] = 0;
    Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
    q.add(s);
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
      int u = q.poll();
      for (int v : graph[u]) {
        if (color[v] == -1) { color[v] = 1 - color[u]; q.add(v); }
        else if (color[v] == color[u]) return false;
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}
Complexity: O(V + E) time, O(V) space

